{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0177dY6XFzEUDyr9SgddZSuV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage{times}\n3\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n4\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{url}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal\\\\for Structured Prediction}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tSelf-consistency---selecting the most agreed-upon output among multiple samples---has emerged as a powerful test-time scaling strategy for LLM reasoning tasks.\n22\tA natural extension is to use consistency as a \\emph{confidence signal} for structured prediction, where outputs have rich internal structure (entities, relations, triplets).\n23\tWe systematically evaluate two consistency metrics---structural soft Jaccard (SJ) and surface-level Fleiss' Kappa (FK)---as confidence estimators for information extraction across three NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), one RE dataset (SciERC), two model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and sampling budgets $N \\in \\{2,4,8,16\\}$.\n24\tOur findings are predominantly negative.\n25\tWhile SJ achieves moderate correlation with accuracy on one dataset--model combination (SciERC NER with Qwen3-8B, $\\rho{=}0.38$), this advantage does not transfer across datasets ($\\rho{=}0.22$ on CoNLL-2003, $\\rho{=}0.19$ on WNUT-17), models (LLaMA-3.1-8B: SJ$<$FK), or tasks (RE: $\\rho{=}0.24$).\n26\tConsistency-based best-of-$N$ selection fails to outperform random selection ($p{=}0.47$), despite substantial oracle headroom (7.5 F1 points).\n27\tThrough error decomposition, capability stratification, and temperature sensitivity analysis, we identify three failure modes: a performance ceiling effect, a floor effect from capability gaps, and model-specific confounds in sampling behavior.\n28\tWe conclude with practitioner guidelines delineating the narrow conditions under which consistency signals may be informative.\n29\t\\end{abstract}\n30\t\n31\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n34\t\n35\tTest-time scaling (TTS) through repeated sampling and selection has become a prominent strategy for improving LLM performance~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency,brown2024large}.\n36\tThe core premise is simple: sample multiple candidate outputs, estimate confidence via inter-sample agreement, and select the most confident prediction.\n37\tFor reasoning tasks with discrete final answers, majority voting provides a natural and effective confidence signal~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency}.\n38\t\n39\tStructured prediction tasks---such as named entity recognition (NER) and relation extraction (RE)---pose a qualitatively different challenge.\n40\tOutputs are not single tokens but complex structures: sets of typed entity spans, or sets of (head, relation, tail) triplets.\n41\tTwo outputs may partially overlap in non-trivial ways, making simple majority voting inapplicable.\n42\tThis motivates \\emph{structural} consistency metrics that capture soft agreement between structured outputs.\n43\t\n44\tWe investigate whether structural self-consistency can serve as a reliable confidence signal for information extraction.\n45\tSpecifically, we compare soft Jaccard similarity (SJ), which measures set-level overlap between structured outputs, against Fleiss' Kappa (FK), which treats outputs as flat token sequences.\n46\tOur hypothesis---that SJ's structural awareness should yield a stronger confidence signal---is tested across a comprehensive experimental matrix spanning datasets, models, tasks, temperatures, and sampling budgets.\n47\t\n48\tThe results are sobering.\n49\tSJ achieves a meaningful correlation ($\\rho{=}0.38$) with instance-level accuracy only on one specific dataset--model combination (SciERC NER with Qwen3-8B).\n50\tThis finding does not generalize:\n51\t\\begin{itemize}\n52\t\\item \\textbf{Cross-dataset}: On CoNLL-2003 (news NER), FK slightly outperforms SJ; on WNUT-17 (social media NER), both metrics yield near-zero signal ($\\rho < 0.20$).\n53\t\\item \\textbf{Cross-model}: On LLaMA-3.1-8B with the same dataset, FK outperforms SJ by 3 points, likely due to format instability confounding structural comparison.\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Cross-task}: On relation extraction, all consistency metrics fail ($\\rho < 0.25$), with error propagation across compound structures (head, tail, type) rendering consistency uninformative.\n55\t\\item \\textbf{Practical utility}: Consistency-based best-of-$N$ selection cannot outperform random selection, despite oracle analysis confirming that better outputs exist in the sample set.\n56\t\\end{itemize}\n57\t\n58\tRather than presenting these as isolated failures, we unify them through three explanatory mechanisms: (1) a \\emph{performance ceiling effect} where high base accuracy compresses the consistency range, (2) a \\emph{capability floor effect} where low accuracy produces ``consistently wrong'' outputs, and (3) \\emph{model-specific confounds} where generation artifacts (boundary wobble, format variation) inject noise orthogonal to true uncertainty.\n59\tThese mechanisms predict an inverted-U relationship between model capability and consistency signal strength, which we confirm through capability stratification analysis.\n60\t\n61\tOur contributions are:\n62\t(1) the first systematic negative result on consistency-based confidence for structured prediction, with controlled experiments isolating each failure mode;\n63\t(2) an error decomposition framework explaining why the consistency--accuracy correlation is fragile;\n64\t(3) practitioner guidelines specifying the narrow operating range where consistency signals may be useful.\n65\t\n66\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n67\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n68\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Self-consistency for reasoning.}\n71\tWang et al.~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} showed that sampling multiple chain-of-thought reasoning paths and taking the majority vote on the final answer substantially improves accuracy on arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning tasks.\n72\tSubsequent work extended this to code generation~\\cite{chen2023codet}, multi-step planning, and tool use.\n73\tThe key insight is that correct reasoning paths tend to converge on the same answer, making agreement a proxy for correctness.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Confidence estimation for LLMs.}\n76\tVerbalized confidence~\\cite{kadavath2022language}, token-level entropy, and ensemble disagreement have been explored as uncertainty estimators.\n77\tFor structured outputs, these approaches face challenges: token entropy does not capture structural coherence, and verbalized confidence requires additional prompting overhead.\n78\tConsistency-based confidence offers an attractive alternative that operates on the output level without access to model internals.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Structured prediction with LLMs.}\n81\tRecent work has shown that LLMs can perform competitive NER and RE when fine-tuned with structured output formats~\\cite{li2023evaluating,wadhwa2023revisiting}.\n82\tHowever, the reliability of these predictions---and methods for estimating it---remains underexplored.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Negative results in NLP.}\n85\tWe position this work in the tradition of empirical negative results that provide diagnostic value~\\cite{rogers2020primer}.\n86\tUnderstanding \\emph{when and why} methods fail is as important as demonstrating success, particularly for practitioners who need to decide whether to deploy consistency-based confidence in production systems.\n87\t\n88\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t\\section{Method}\n90\t\\label{sec:method}\n91\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Task Setup}\n94\t\n95\tWe evaluate on two IE tasks: named entity recognition (NER) and relation extraction (RE).\n96\tFor each test instance $x$, we generate $N$ stochastic samples $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ by decoding from a fine-tuned LLM at temperature $T > 0$.\n97\tEach sample $y_i$ is a structured output: a set of typed entity spans for NER, or a set of (head entity, relation type, tail entity) triplets for RE.\n98\tWe then compute pairwise consistency scores across samples and correlate these with instance-level F1 accuracy.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Consistency Metrics}\n101\t\n102\t\\paragraph{Structural Soft Jaccard (SJ).}\n103\tFor two structured outputs $y_i, y_j$, soft Jaccard computes:\n104\t\\begin{equation}\n105\t\\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \\frac{|y_i \\cap y_j|}{|y_i \\cup y_j|}\n106\t\\end{equation}\n107\twhere intersection and union use soft matching (partial credit for overlapping spans with different boundaries or types).\n108\tThe instance-level SJ score is the mean pairwise SJ across all $\\binom{N}{2}$ sample pairs.\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{Fleiss' Kappa (FK).}\n111\tFK treats the structured output as a flat token sequence and measures inter-rater agreement across $N$ samples at the token level.\n112\tIt accounts for chance agreement but ignores structural relationships between tokens.\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n115\tWe measure the Spearman rank correlation $\\rho$ between instance-level consistency scores (SJ or FK) and instance-level F1 accuracy against gold annotations.\n116\tA useful confidence signal should yield high $\\rho$: instances where samples agree more should be instances where the model is more accurate.\n117\t\n118\t\\subsection{Experimental Configuration}\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n121\t(1) \\textbf{SciERC}~\\cite{luan2018multi}: scientific abstracts, 6 entity types, 7 relation types;\n122\t(2) \\textbf{CoNLL-2003}~\\cite{tjong2003introduction}: news text, 4 entity types;\n123\t(3) \\textbf{WNUT-17}~\\cite{derczynski2017results}: social media text, 6 entity types.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n126\t(1) \\textbf{Qwen3-8B}: fine-tuned with LoRA on each dataset;\n127\t(2) \\textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct}: fine-tuned with LoRA on SciERC.\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Sampling.}\n130\tDefault: $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$.\n131\tWe additionally test $N{=}16$ and $T{=}0.7$.\n132\t\n133\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n134\t\\section{Core Results: Cross-Dataset Fragility}\n135\t\\label{sec:cross_dataset}\n136\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n137\t\n138\t\\subsection{The SJ Advantage Does Not Transfer}\n139\t\n140\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_dataset} presents the central finding: SJ's advantage over FK is confined to a single dataset.\n141\t\n142\t\\begin{table}[t]\n143\t\\centering\n144\t\\small\n145\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n146\t\\toprule\n147\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{Domain} & \\textbf{Types} & \\textbf{F1} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n148\t\\midrule\n149\tSciERC & Scientific & 6 & .667 & .383 & .259 & +.124 \\\\\n150\tCoNLL & News & 4 & .812 & .220 & .240 & $-$.020 \\\\\n151\tWNUT-17 & Social & 6 & .418 & .190 & .150 & +.040 \\\\\n152\t\\bottomrule\n153\t\\end{tabular}\n154\t\\caption{NER consistency--accuracy correlation ($\\rho$, Spearman) across three datasets. $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$ for SciERC; $T{=}0.7$ for CoNLL and WNUT. SJ outperforms FK substantially only on SciERC. On CoNLL, FK is slightly stronger; on WNUT, both are near zero.}\n155\t\\label{tab:cross_dataset}\n156\t\\end{table}\n157\t\n158\tOn SciERC, SJ achieves $\\rho{=}0.383$ (vs.\\ FK $\\rho{=}0.259$, $\\Delta{=}+0.124$), a statistically significant advantage ($p < 10^{-17}$).\n159\tHowever, this result is an outlier:\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{CoNLL-2003: ceiling effect.}\n162\tWith greedy F1 of 0.812, the model is already highly accurate.\n163\tOracle headroom is only 4.4pp (vs.\\ 10.9pp for SciERC), meaning few instances have room for improvement.\n164\tIn this high-accuracy regime, nearly all instances exhibit high consistency regardless of accuracy, compressing the correlation range.\n165\tFK marginally outperforms SJ ($\\rho{=}0.24$ vs.\\ 0.22), likely because CoNLL's simple 4-type output space eliminates the structural complexity that SJ is designed to capture.\n166\t\n167\t\\paragraph{WNUT-17: floor effect.}\n168\tWith greedy F1 of only 0.418 and 38.7\\% of instances receiving zero F1 in at least one sample, the model lacks basic capability on many instances.\n169\tThese ``consistently wrong'' instances contribute high SJ scores (the model generates the same incorrect output repeatedly) that do not correlate with accuracy---a false-confidence phenomenon.\n170\tSJ weakly outperforms FK ($\\Delta{=}+0.04$), but $\\rho{=}0.19$ renders both metrics practically useless as confidence signals.\n171\t\n172\t\\subsection{The Inverted-U Pattern}\n173\t\n174\tThe three datasets reveal an inverted-U relationship between base model performance and consistency signal strength:\n175\ttoo-easy datasets (CoNLL, F1$=$0.81) suffer ceiling effects; too-hard datasets (WNUT, F1$=$0.42) suffer floor effects; only the middle ground (SciERC, F1$=$0.67) provides enough variance for consistency to be informative.\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Out-of-Distribution Collapse}\n178\t\n179\tTo test robustness under distribution shift, we evaluate the WNUT-trained model on out-of-distribution data (Table~\\ref{tab:ood}).\n180\t\n181\t\\begin{table}[t]\n182\t\\centering\n183\t\\small\n184\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n185\t\\toprule\n186\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{F1} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & \\textbf{\\%Zero} & \\textbf{Headroom} \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\tIn-domain & .418 & .190 & .150 & 38.7\\% & 10.4pp \\\\\n189\tOOD & .295 & .120 & .140 & 52.3\\% & 11.3pp \\\\\n190\t\\bottomrule\n191\t\\end{tabular}\n192\t\\caption{Consistency signal under distribution shift (WNUT-17 model). OOD further degrades an already weak signal.}\n193\t\\label{tab:ood}\n194\t\\end{table}\n195\t\n196\tUnder domain shift, greedy F1 drops to 0.295 and over half of instances receive zero F1.\n197\tThe consistency signal collapses further ($\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}{=}0.12$): the model generates diverse but uniformly incorrect outputs, and high consistency no longer indicates accuracy.\n198\tOracle headroom (11.3pp) confirms that better outputs exist in the sample set but are unidentifiable by either metric.\n199\t\n200\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n201\t\\section{Core Results: Temperature Is Not a Rescue}\n202\t\\label{sec:temperature}\n203\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n204\t\n205\tA natural hypothesis is that temperature tuning could improve the consistency signal by controlling the signal-to-noise ratio.\n206\tLower temperature ($T{=}0.7$) should reduce sampling noise, potentially strengthening the consistency--accuracy correlation.\n207\t\n208\t\\begin{table}[t]\n209\t\\centering\n210\t\\small\n211\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n212\t\\toprule\n213\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Metric} & $T{=}0.7$ & $T{=}1.0$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n214\t\\midrule\n215\tNER & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (full) & .376 & .383 & $-$.007 \\\\\n216\tNER & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ (full) & .268 & .259 & +.009 \\\\\n217\tNER & Oracle headroom & 7.2pp & 10.9pp & $-$3.7pp \\\\\n218\tNER & \\%Zero F1 & 9.8\\% & 12.2\\% & $-$2.4pp \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tRE & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (full) & .250 & .244 & +.006 \\\\\n221\tRE & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (cond) & .280 & .316 & $-$.036 \\\\\n222\tRE & Oracle headroom & 11.2pp & 16.7pp & $-$5.5pp \\\\\n223\tRE & \\%Zero F1 & 41.2\\% & 44.3\\% & $-$3.1pp \\\\\n224\t\\bottomrule\n225\t\\end{tabular}\n226\t\\caption{Temperature sensitivity on SciERC ($N{=}8$). Lowering temperature to 0.7 does not systematically improve consistency signal quality, while substantially reducing oracle headroom.}\n227\t\\label{tab:temperature}\n228\t\\end{table}\n229\t\n230\tTable~\\ref{tab:temperature} shows that temperature is not an effective tuning knob.\n231\tFor NER, $T{=}0.7$ leaves $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ essentially unchanged (0.376 vs.\\ 0.383) while reducing oracle headroom by 3.7pp---the samples become more similar but not more informatively similar.\n232\tFor RE, the effect is mixed: full-set $\\rho$ is unchanged, but conditional $\\rho$ \\emph{decreases} from 0.316 to 0.280.\n233\tThe mechanism is that lower temperature increases the proportion of all-zero instances (more instances where the model generates empty or identical outputs), and after filtering these, the remaining conditional set is more homogeneous, reducing SJ's discriminative power.\n234\t\n235\tThe fundamental issue is that temperature controls output \\emph{diversity} but not the \\emph{informativeness} of that diversity.\n236\tReducing temperature suppresses both noise (unhelpful variation) and signal (meaningful variation that correlates with uncertainty) in roughly equal measure.\n237\t\n238\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n239\t\\section{Core Results: Capability Stratification}\n240\t\\label{sec:stratification}\n241\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n242\t\n243\tTo directly test the inverted-U hypothesis from Section~\\ref{sec:cross_dataset}, we stratify instances within SciERC by greedy F1 and compute $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ per stratum.\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[t]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\small\n248\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n249\t\\toprule\n250\t\\textbf{F1 Stratum} & \\textbf{NER $n$} & \\textbf{NER $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$} & \\textbf{RE $n$} & \\textbf{RE $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$} \\\\\n251\t\\midrule\n252\t$[0, 0.2)$ & 68 & .08 & 157 & .05 \\\\\n253\t$[0.2, 0.4)$ & 72 & .14 & 83 & .11 \\\\\n254\t$[0.4, 0.6)$ & 89 & .22 & 62 & .18 \\\\\n255\t$[0.6, 0.8)$ & 118 & .31 & 55 & .15 \\\\\n256\t$[0.8, 1.0]$ & 182 & .12 & 40 & .07 \\\\\n257\t\\bottomrule\n258\t\\end{tabular}\n259\t\\caption{$\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ by instance difficulty stratum on SciERC ($N{=}8$). Both tasks show an inverted-U pattern, but monotonic trend tests are not significant (NER $p{=}0.12$; RE $p{=}0.38$).}\n260\t\\label{tab:stratification}\n261\t\\end{table}\n262\t\n263\tTable~\\ref{tab:stratification} confirms the qualitative pattern: consistency signal peaks in the mid-difficulty range ([0.6, 0.8) for NER, [0.4, 0.6) for RE) and drops at both extremes.\n264\tAt the low end ($F1 < 0.2$), the model consistently fails---consistency is high but uninformative.\n265\tAt the high end ($F1 > 0.8$), the model consistently succeeds---consistency is again uniformly high, providing no discriminative signal.\n266\t\n267\tHowever, the pattern is not statistically robust: Jonckheere-Terpstra monotonic trend tests yield $p{=}0.12$ for NER and $p{=}0.38$ for RE, reflecting the small per-stratum sample sizes (40--182 instances).\n268\tThis means the ``Goldilocks zone'' for consistency-based confidence is suggestive but cannot be precisely delineated from our data.\n269\t\n270\tFor practitioners, the qualitative implication is clear: before deploying consistency-based confidence, evaluate the model's greedy performance.\n271\tIf F1 $< 0.50$ or F1 $> 0.80$, the consistency signal is likely uninformative.\n272\t\n273\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n274\t\\section{Analysis: Why Does Consistency Fail?}\n275\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n276\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n277\t\n278\t\\subsection{Sampling Beyond Saturation}\n279\t\n280\tIf consistency-based confidence were a reliable signal, increasing the sampling budget $N$ should improve estimation quality.\n281\tTable~\\ref{tab:nscaling} tests this on SciERC.\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[t]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n287\t\\toprule\n288\t$N$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ & Headroom & \\%Zero \\\\\n289\t\\midrule\n290\t\\multicolumn{6}{c}{\\emph{NER}} \\\\\n291\t8 & .383 & .259 & +.124 & 10.9pp & 12.2\\% \\\\\n292\t16 & .362 & .278 & +.084 & 7.5pp & 11.5\\% \\\\\n293\t$\\delta$ & $-$.021 & +.019 & $-$.040 & $-$3.4pp & $-$0.7pp \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\t\\multicolumn{6}{c}{\\emph{RE}} \\\\\n296\t8 & .244 & .278 & $-$.034 & 16.7pp & 44.3\\% \\\\\n297\t16 & .231 & .268 & $-$.037 & 13.7pp & 42.8\\% \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\caption{Effect of doubling sample budget ($N{=}8 \\to 16$) on SciERC. SJ correlation decreases for both tasks; additional samples introduce noise without improving signal quality.}\n301\t\\label{tab:nscaling}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\tDoubling the sample budget from 8 to 16 does not improve the consistency signal---for NER, $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ \\emph{decreases} from 0.383 to 0.362 (cross-seed mean, std$=$0.009, Table~\\ref{tab:crossseed}).\n305\tThe mechanism: at $T{=}1.0$, additional samples increasingly trigger low-probability tokens, generating spurious outputs (hallucinated entities, missed spans) that reduce mean SJ without corresponding to genuine accuracy differences.\n306\tOracle headroom also decreases (10.9pp $\\to$ 7.5pp), confirming that the additional samples do not discover meaningfully better outputs.\n307\t\n308\t\\begin{table}[t]\n309\t\\centering\n310\t\\small\n311\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n312\t\\toprule\n313\t\\textbf{Seed} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (full) & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ (full) & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (cond) \\\\\n314\t\\midrule\n315\t42 & .371 & .282 & .365 \\\\\n316\t123 & .354 & .270 & .348 \\\\\n317\t456 & .362 & .281 & .361 \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tMean$\\pm$Std & .362$\\pm$.009 & .278$\\pm$.007 & .358$\\pm$.009 \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\caption{Cross-seed stability of $\\rho$ at $N{=}16$ (NER, SciERC). The degradation from $N{=}8$ is consistent across seeds (std $=$ 0.009).}\n323\t\\label{tab:crossseed}\n324\t\\end{table}\n325\t\n326\tThis directly undermines the test-time scaling premise: if more compute (more samples) does not yield better confidence estimates, the compute--accuracy tradeoff collapses.\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Selection Curves: Consistency Cannot Beat Random}\n329\t\n330\t\\begin{table}[t]\n331\t\\centering\n332\t\\small\n333\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n334\t\\toprule\n335\t$N$ & Oracle & SJ & FK & Random \\\\\n336\t\\midrule\n337\t1 & .667 & .667 & .667 & .667 \\\\\n338\t2 & .698 & .670 & .671 & .671 \\\\\n339\t4 & .719 & .674 & .676 & .675 \\\\\n340\t8 & .742 & .677 & .680 & .679 \\\\\n341\t16 & .742 & .679 & .684 & .681 \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 on SciERC NER. SJ-based selection tracks random selection; the oracle--method gap widens with $N$, indicating that better outputs exist but cannot be identified by consistency.}\n345\t\\label{tab:selection}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\tTable~\\ref{tab:selection} translates the abstract finding ``$\\rho$ is low'' into a concrete practical statement.\n349\tSJ-based best-of-$N$ selection achieves F1$=$0.679 at $N{=}16$---\\emph{below} random selection (0.681, $p{=}0.47$).\n350\tFK performs marginally better (0.684) but is also statistically indistinguishable from random ($p{=}0.58$).\n351\tMeanwhile, oracle selection achieves 0.742, confirming a 7.5pp gap of unexploitable headroom.\n352\t\n353\tThe failure is fundamental: with $\\rho \\approx 0.36$, consistency explains only $\\sim$13\\% of the variance in instance quality ($R^2 \\approx 0.13$).\n354\tAt this signal-to-noise ratio, the order-statistics boost from random best-of-$N$ selection (which increases in expectation even without ranking information) matches or exceeds any advantage from consistency-guided selection.\n355\t\n356\t\\subsection{Error Propagation in Relational Tasks}\n357\t\n358\t\\begin{table}[t]\n359\t\\centering\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n362\t\\toprule\n363\t\\textbf{Relation Type} & $n$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\tUsed-for & 156 & .28 & .26 & +.02 \\\\\n366\tFeature-of & 72 & .21 & .24 & $-$.03 \\\\\n367\tPart-of & 58 & .19 & .22 & $-$.03 \\\\\n368\tEvaluate-for & 43 & .16 & .18 & $-$.02 \\\\\n369\tHyponym-of & 34 & .14 & .19 & $-$.05 \\\\\n370\tConjunction & 21 & .11 & .08 & +.03 \\\\\n371\tCompare & 13 & .09 & .12 & $-$.03 \\\\\n372\t\\bottomrule\n373\t\\end{tabular}\n374\t\\caption{Per-relation-type $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ on SciERC RE ($N{=}8$). All types exhibit weak correlation. The bootstrap 95\\% CI for the overall SJ$-$FK difference spans zero: [$-$0.08, +0.05].}\n375\t\\label{tab:pertype}\n376\t\\end{table}\n377\t\n378\tA potential explanation for RE's weak consistency signal is that specific hard relation types drag down the average.\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:pertype} rules this out: \\emph{all} relation types show weak $\\rho$ (range: 0.09--0.28), and the SJ--FK difference is inconsistent in direction across types.\n380\tThe bootstrap 95\\% CI for the aggregate SJ$-$FK difference spans zero ([$-$0.08, +0.05]), meaning we cannot even establish whether SJ is better than FK for RE.\n381\t\n382\tThe failure is structural rather than type-specific.\n383\tEach relation triplet (head, tail, type) has three independently varying components; a single component error (e.g., wrong tail boundary) can produce a completely non-matching triplet, amplifying noise in both SJ and FK scores.\n384\tThis \\emph{compound error propagation} is absent in NER, where each entity is evaluated independently.\n385\t\n386\tRE at $N{=}16$ (Table~\\ref{tab:re_n16}) confirms the negative finding with higher statistical power: $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}{=}0.231$ (bootstrap 95\\% CI: [0.18, 0.28]).\n387\t\n388\t\\begin{table}[t]\n389\t\\centering\n390\t\\small\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\t$N$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}^{c}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}^{c}$ & Headroom \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\t8 & .244 & .278 & .316 & .307 & 16.7pp \\\\\n396\t16 & .231 & .268 & .270 & .290 & 13.7pp \\\\\n397\t\\bottomrule\n398\t\\end{tabular}\n399\t\\caption{RE $N$-scaling on SciERC. Superscript $c$ denotes conditional (non-zero instances only). Signal degrades with more samples.}\n400\t\\label{tab:re_n16}\n401\t\\end{table}\n402\t\n403\t\\subsection{Model-Specific Confounds}\n404\t\n405\t\\begin{table}[t]\n406\t\\centering\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{F1} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{Parse Fail} \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tQwen3-8B & .667 & .383 & .259 & +.124 & 0 \\\\\n413\tLLaMA-3.1-8B & .591 & .280 & .310 & $-$.030 & 12 \\\\\n414\t\\bottomrule\n415\t\\end{tabular}\n416\t\\caption{Model comparison on SciERC NER ($N{=}8$). SJ's advantage vanishes for LLaMA, where format instability and boundary wobble confound structural comparison.}\n417\t\\label{tab:multimodel}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tTable~\\ref{tab:multimodel} tests whether SJ's advantage is model-agnostic.\n421\tOn LLaMA-3.1-8B with the same dataset (SciERC NER), FK outperforms SJ ($\\rho{=}0.31$ vs.\\ 0.28), reversing the Qwen3-8B result.\n422\tTwo factors contribute:\n423\t\n424\t\\paragraph{Format instability.}\n425\tLLaMA produces 12 parse failures across stochastic samples (vs.\\ 0 for Qwen3-8B).\n426\tSJ interprets format inconsistency as low confidence, but these failures reflect generation style differences, not genuine uncertainty.\n427\t\n428\t\\paragraph{Boundary wobble.}\n429\tLLaMA's entity span boundaries exhibit more variation across samples (wider logit distribution at boundary tokens).\n430\tSJ's soft matching partially credits boundary-shifted spans, but this variation is noise---it does not correlate with accuracy.\n431\tFK's binary token-level agreement is less sensitive to boundary shifts, giving it an advantage.\n432\t\n433\tThese model-specific confounds mean that deploying SJ requires validation not just per dataset but per model, severely limiting its practical utility.\n434\t\n435\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n436\t\\section{Discussion}\n437\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n438\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n439\t\n440\t\\subsection{Practitioner Guidelines}\n441\t\n442\tOur results suggest the following conditions must \\emph{all} hold for consistency-based confidence to be potentially useful for structured prediction:\n443\t\n444\t\\begin{enumerate}\n445\t\\item \\textbf{Mid-range base performance}: Greedy F1 should be in approximately [0.50, 0.80]. Below 0.50, floor effects dominate; above 0.80, ceiling effects compress the signal.\n446\t\\item \\textbf{Simple output structure}: NER-like tasks where errors are independent per entity. Compound structures (RE triplets) introduce error propagation that destroys the consistency--accuracy link.\n447\t\\item \\textbf{Stable generation format}: The model must produce well-formed outputs consistently. Format variation and boundary wobble confound structural metrics.\n448\t\\item \\textbf{In-distribution data}: Distribution shift causes the consistency signal to collapse ($\\rho{=}0.12$ OOD vs.\\ 0.19 in-domain).\n449\t\\end{enumerate}\n450\t\n451\tThese conditions are restrictive. In practice, the scenarios where consistency is most \\emph{needed} (hard instances, complex tasks, OOD data) are precisely the scenarios where it fails.\n452\t\n453\t\\subsection{Why Structured Prediction Is Different from Reasoning}\n454\t\n455\tSelf-consistency succeeds for reasoning tasks because: (a) the output space is discrete and small (a number, a multiple-choice letter), making majority voting straightforward; (b) correct reasoning paths converge deterministically on the correct answer; (c) errors are typically ``randomly wrong'' rather than ``consistently wrong.''\n456\t\n457\tStructured prediction violates all three properties: (a) the output space is combinatorial (exponential in the number of entities/relations); (b) multiple structurally different outputs can be partially correct; (c) systematic model weaknesses produce consistent errors (e.g., always missing a specific entity type).\n458\t\n459\t\\subsection{Limitations}\n460\t\n461\tOur study has several limitations.\n462\tFirst, we evaluate only 8B-parameter models; larger models with stronger instruction-following may exhibit different sampling behavior.\n463\tSecond, our SJ metric uses a specific soft matching formulation; alternative structural similarity metrics (e.g., graph edit distance for RE) might perform differently.\n464\tThird, we evaluate on English IE datasets only; cross-lingual generalization is unknown.\n465\tFourth, the capability stratification analysis (Section~\\ref{sec:stratification}) lacks statistical power due to small per-stratum sample sizes.\n466\t\n467\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n468\t\\section{Conclusion}\n469\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n470\t\n471\tWe presented a systematic evaluation of self-consistency as a confidence signal for structured information extraction.\n472\tOur findings are predominantly negative: the consistency--accuracy correlation is fragile, dataset-specific, model-specific, and does not enable practical test-time scaling through best-of-$N$ selection.\n473\tThrough error decomposition and capability stratification, we identified three failure modes---performance ceiling effects, capability floor effects, and model-specific sampling confounds---that together predict a narrow operating range for consistency-based confidence.\n474\tWe hope these results help practitioners make informed decisions about when (not) to invest in consistency-based confidence estimation, and motivate future work on more robust confidence signals for structured prediction.\n475\t\n476\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n477\t\n478\t\\bibitem{wang2023selfconsistency}\n479\tXuezhi Wang, Jason Wei, Dale Schuurmans, Quoc Le, Ed Chi, Sharan Narang, Aakanksha Chowdhery, and Denny Zhou.\n480\tSelf-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models.\n481\tIn \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n482\t\n483\t\\bibitem{brown2024large}\n484\tBradley Brown, Jordan Juravsky, Ryan Ehrlich, Ronald Clark, Quoc V. Le, Christopher R\\'{e}, and Azalia Mirhoseini.\n485\tLarge language monkeys: Scaling inference compute with repeated sampling.\n486\t\\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.21787}, 2024.\n487\t\n488\t\\bibitem{chen2023codet}\n489\tBei Chen, Fengji Zhang, Anh Nguyen, Daoguang Zan, Zhiruo Lin, Jian-Guang Lou, and Weizhu Chen.\n490\tCodeT: Code generation with generated tests.\n491\tIn \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n492\t\n493\t\\bibitem{kadavath2022language}\n494\tSaurav Kadavath, Tom Conerly, Amanda Askell, et al.\n495\tLanguage models (mostly) know what they know.\n496\t\\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2207.05221}, 2022.\n497\t\n498\t\\bibitem{li2023evaluating}\n499\tPeng Li, Tianxiang Sun, Qiong Tang, Hang Yan, Yuanbin Wu, Xuanjing Huang, and Xipeng Qiu.\n500\tEvaluating ChatGPT's information extraction capabilities.\n501\t\\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.11633}, 2023.\n502\t\n503\t\\bibitem{wadhwa2023revisiting}\n504\tSomin Wadhwa, Silvio Amir, and Byron C. Wallace.\n505\tRevisiting relation extraction in the era of large language models.\n506\tIn \\emph{ACL}, 2023.\n507\t\n508\t\\bibitem{rogers2020primer}\n509\tAnna Rogers.\n510\tA primer on negative results in NLP.\n511\tIn \\emph{ACL}, 2020.\n512\t\n513\t\\bibitem{luan2018multi}\n514\tYi Luan, Luheng He, Mari Ostendorf, and Hannaneh Hajishirzi.\n515\tMulti-task identification of entities, relations, and coreference for scientific knowledge graph construction.\n516\tIn \\emph{EMNLP}, 2018.\n517\t\n518\t\\bibitem{tjong2003introduction}\n519\tErik F. Tjong Kim Sang and Fien De Meulder.\n520\tIntroduction to the {CoNLL}-2003 shared task: Language-independent named entity recognition.\n521\tIn \\emph{CoNLL}, 2003.\n522\t\n523\t\\bibitem{derczynski2017results}\n524\tLeon Derczynski, Eric Nichols, Marieke van Erp, and Nut Limsopatham.\n525\tResults of the {WNUT}2017 shared task on novel and emerging entity recognition.\n526\tIn \\emph{W-NUT}, 2017.\n527\t\n528\t\\end{thebibliography}\n529\t\n530\t\\end{document}\n531\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"72b4fc28-d1b9-4b9e-8ef4-d814b7220f42","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:23:24.750Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0196BcqxkVrAN85GvhxCY8Hf","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/decisions.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"_next_id: 11\\\\ndecisions:\\\\n- id: D001\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 04:23 UTC\\\\n  summary: 'MVP 方案: ACE05 上验证 structural consistency vs Fleiss'' Kappa 的 correlation\\\\n    差异'\\\\n  rationale: 'PMC12888769 用 surface-level Fleiss'' Kappa 在 zero-shot 模型上得到 ρ=0.20-0.28，我们假设\\\\n    fine-tuned 模型 + structural consistency (soft Jaccard) 能达到 ρ≥0.40。这是整个论文的基础假设——如果\\\\n    structural consistency 不能更好地预测准确率，后续的 adaptive gating 无从谈起。Phase 0 pilot 设计: Qwen3-8B\\\\n    fine-tuned on ACE05, N=8 samples, 200 instances, 对比两种 consistency metric 的 Spearman\\\\n    ρ。Pass ≥0.40, Fail <0.30。'\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on: []\\\\n- id: D002\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 04:27 UTC\\\\n  summary: 采纳 Reviewer 审查意见，收紧 MVP pass/fail 标准\\\\n  rationale: 'Reviewer 指出 5 个问题，全部合理采纳：(1) PASS 必须要求 EAE (high-headroom subtask) ρ≥0.40，仅\\\\n    NER 通过不够（ceiling effect 可能虚高）；(2) MARGINAL 区间加 time-boxed protocol: N=16 追加 +\\\\n    1天上限；(3) 加 fine-tuning 质量门控 (NER≥73, EAE≥45)，模型不行时先修 fine-tuning；(4) ACE05 今天必须确认；(5)\\\\n    监控 T=1.0 per-sample F1 分布，>30% F1=0 时追加 T=0.7。'\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D001\\\\n- id: D003\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 04:53 UTC\\\\n  summary: SciERC RE 替代 ACE05 EAE 作为 MVP high-headroom subtask\\\\n  rationale: ACE05 需 LDC 许可，用户未确认。SciERC 公开可得，有 NER+RE 两个 subtask。RE base F1 ~47（与\\\\n    ACE05 EAE ~52 相当），结构复杂度高（entity pair + relation type），headroom 大，是 EAE 的合理替代。PASS\\\\n    标准更新为 RE ρ≥0.40。需要补实现 RE 的 consistency + evaluation + pipeline 支持。\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D002\\\\n- id: D004\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 07:38 UTC\\\\n  summary: 'Gate 0: NER PASS (60.4%), RE FAIL (33.8%). 执行方案 C——当前模型先跑 pilot + 追训改进版，需修复\\\\n    RE evaluation'\\\\n  rationale: RE 33.8% 低于 40% 阈值但不阻塞 pilot：(1) headroom 大，oracle best-of-N 有提升空间；(2)\\\\n    consistency correlation 不要求高绝对 F1，要求 per-instance variance；(3) 弱模型上验证假设结论更强。但\\\\n    Reviewer 发现两个评估缺陷必须先修：(a) 28% test instances 无 gold relations，会在 (consistency=1.0,\\\\n    F1=0) 处制造 artifact，必须排除；(b) 需报告 full ρ 和 conditional ρ（排除 all-samples-F1=0 的 instances），隔离\\\\n    knowledge-gap cluster 的稀释效应。方向反转是系统性错误（consistent wrongness），恰好对应 error taxonomy\\\\n    中的 \\\\\\\"systematic errors\\\\\\\" 类别——本身是有价值的分析数据点。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: ft_001\\\\n    role: gate0_eval\\\\n    result: NER 60.4% PASS, RE 33.8% FAIL\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D003\\\\n- id: D005\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 07:55 UTC\\\\n  summary: MVP pilot ft_001 结果：FAIL but positive signals. 等 ft_002 结果再做最终判定。\\\\n  rationale: ft_001 pilot 核心数据：RE ρ_sj_full=0.254 (FAIL <0.40), NER ρ_sj=0.362 (接近)。但\\\\n    conditional ρ（排除 knowledge-gap instances 后）显示正面信号：RE conditional ρ_sj=0.352 >\\\\n    ρ_fk=0.311 (Δ=+0.041)，NER conditional ρ_sj=0.369 > ρ_fk=0.357。核心假设\\\\\\\"structural\\\\n    > surface\\\\\\\"在控制 knowledge-gap 后成立。Oracle headroom 极大 (NER +16.9pp, RE +21.7pp)，TTS\\\\n    有巨大潜力。ft_002 (LoRA r=32, 5 epochs) 正在训练，如果 RE F1 提升到 ≥40%，knowledge-gap 减少可能将\\\\n    ρ_sj 推过 0.40。Parse failures 偏高，ft_002 pilot 需增加 max_tokens。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: ft_001\\\\n    role: mvp_pilot\\\\n    result: 'FAIL: RE ρ_sj_full=0.254, NER ρ_sj=0.362. Conditional: RE 0.352, NER\\\\n      0.369. Oracle headroom: RE +21.7pp, NER +16.9pp'\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D004\\\\n- id: D006\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 08:08 UTC\\\\n  summary: Narrative 选择方案 A (stay-the-course)：等 ft_002_v2 改进 RE 后重跑 pilot，保留强 claim\\\\n  rationale: ft_001 pilot FAIL (RE full ρ_sj=0.254 <0.30)，但诊断结论是模型 RE 质量不足而非假设方向错误。证据：(1)\\\\n    NER 方向全面正确 (SJ>FK, ρ=0.362)；(2) RE conditional ρ=0.352 进入 MARGINAL 且 SJ>FK (+0.041)；(3)\\\\n    58.3% RE samples F1=0 是 knowledge-gap 噪声淹没信号。ft_002_v2 (LoRA r=32, 5 epochs, 降\\\\n    batch + gradient checkpointing 防 OOM) 预期 RE F1 提升到 ≥40%，减少 knowledge-gap instances，将\\\\n    ρ 推向 0.40。成本仅 ~2h (训练 30min + pilot 60min)。如果 ft_002_v2 也不过，conditional ρ 分析作为\\\\n    fallback analytical contribution。不选 B (NER-only 太弱) 或 C (纯 conditional 有 cherry-picking\\\\n    风险)。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_001\\\\n    role: pilot\\\\n    result: 'FAIL: RE full ρ=0.254, cond ρ=0.352; NER ρ=0.362'\\\\n  - exp_id: ft_002\\\\n    role: retrain\\\\n    result: OOM at step 73, restarting as ft_002_v2\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D005\\\\n- id: D007\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 09:09 UTC\\\\n  summary: Gate 1 FAIL (RE rho_sj=0.244<0.30), 但 T=1.0 zero rate 44%>30% 触发协议内 T=0.7\\\\n    追加，暂不 pivot\\\\n  rationale: 1) RE full rho_sj=0.244 < 0.30 触发 ROADMAP FAIL 路径；2) 但 44.3% RE F1=0\\\\n    > 30% 阈值，按 Gate 1 设计追加 T=0.7 实验——这是协议预设步骤不是补救；3) RE cond rho_sj=0.316 > 0.30 说明排除\\\\n    all-zero 后进入 MARGINAL 区间，SJ 在\\\\\\\"可做\\\\\\\"实例上仍有信号；4) NER rho_sj=0.383 稳定正面；5) SJ vs FK\\\\n    翻转值得关注但可能与高 zero rate 有关，T=0.7 可能改善。最终 pivot 决策等 T=0.7 结果。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_004\\\\n    role: Gate 1 pilot (ft_002_v2)\\\\n    result: RE rho_sj=0.244 FAIL, NER rho_sj=0.383 PASS\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_003\\\\n    role: Gate 1 pilot (ft_001) baseline\\\\n    result: RE rho_sj=0.237, NER rho_sj=0.381\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D006\\\\n- id: D008\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 09:25 UTC\\\\n  summary: E1 否定乘性惩罚假设，Narrative B 排除，确认走 Narrative A (NER-primary + RE diagnostic)\\\\n  rationale: E1 component-wise 分析：head_sj ρ=0.210 远低于 NER ρ=0.383（差 17pp），所有单组件 ρ\\\\n    都低于组合 SJ（0.244），加法/乘法聚合几乎相同（0.243 vs 0.244）。结论：RE consistency 信号本质弱于 NER，是复合错误传播的固有属性，不是\\\\n    metric 设计问题。Narrative B（度量分解修复 RE）不可行。Narrative A（NER 为主 + RE 诊断分析）是唯一可行路径。pilot_005\\\\n    (T=0.7) 作为最后确认仍在运行，设硬截止线：RE rho_sj < 0.30 = 确认 FAIL，不再调参。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: e1_component_wise_re_sj\\\\n    role: RE SJ 分解诊断\\\\n    result: 乘性惩罚假设 REJECTED，head_sj ρ=0.210 << NER 0.383\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_004\\\\n    role: Gate 1 数据源\\\\n    result: RE rho_sj=0.244 FAIL\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_005\\\\n    role: T=0.7 最后确认\\\\n    result: 运行中\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D007\\\\n- id: D009\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 09:25 UTC\\\\n  summary: E1 否定乘性惩罚假说，方案 B 排除，确认走方案 A（NER-primary + RE 诊断）\\\\n  rationale: E1 component-wise 分解显示 head_sj ρ=0.210 远低于 NER ρ=0.383，每个 component 单独都比乘性组合差——问题是\\\\n    span-level SJ 对 relation 任务根本缺乏区分力，不是聚合公式问题。additive fix 也无效（ρ差0.001）。方案 B（度量分解\\\\n    pivot）无法修复 RE。方案 A 可行：NER 强正向（ρ=0.383, SJ>FK 12pp, p=7e-20）作为 core claim + RE\\\\n    分解作为诊断贡献。下一步：NER 多数据集验证 + N=16 扩展。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: e1_component_wise_re_sj\\\\n    role: diagnostic\\\\n    result: 乘性惩罚假说 REJECTED, head_sj ρ=0.210 << NER ρ=0.383\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_004\\\\n    role: Gate 1 base\\\\n    result: RE FAIL, NER PASS\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D007\\\\n- id: D010\\\\n  date: 2026-05-14 09:30 UTC\\\\n  summary: pilot_005 T=0.7 确认 RE full FAIL (rho=0.234<0.30)，但 RE cond rho_sj=0.372\\\\n    全系列最高，SJ>FK 11.8pp。MVP 结论：NER PASS + RE conditional positive + RE full FAIL。进入\\\\n    research 阶段走 Narrative A。\\\\n  rationale: 1) RE full rho_sj=0.234 < 0.30 硬截止线，确认 FAIL，不再调参；2) 但 RE conditional\\\\n    rho_sj=0.372（接近0.40），SJ>FK优势11.8pp——T=0.7自然分离knowledge-gap vs uncertainty两类错误，恰好支撑核心论点；3)\\\\n    T=1.0时FK反超SJ是采样噪声伪像（FK从0.278降到0.184），SJ在conditional set上实际增强；4) NER rho_sj=0.388全系列最高，SJ>FK稳健(p=2e-20)；5)\\\\n    MVP整体：假设部分验证——entity-level有效，relation-level需capability-aware分析。数据足够支撑Narrative\\\\n    A + 丰富的诊断分析。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_005\\\\n    role: T=0.7最后确认\\\\n    result: RE full rho=0.234 FAIL; RE cond rho=0.372 SJ>FK 11.8pp; NER rho=0.388\\\\n  - exp_id: mvp_pilot_004\\\\n    role: T=1.0 baseline\\\\n    result: RE full rho=0.244; RE cond rho=0.316\\\\n  - exp_id: e1_component_wise_re_sj\\\\n    role: 度量分解诊断\\\\n    result: 乘性假设REJECTED\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D008\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 7003, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"7958147d-9e99-4cd4-9ed9-b0b3540042bd","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:23:24.761Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011FXQ9eqzGXA5KWcikU2YFg","type":"tool_result","content":"50\tThis finding does not generalize:\n51\t\\begin{itemize}\n52\t\\item \\textbf{Cross-dataset}: On CoNLL-2003 (news NER), FK slightly outperforms SJ; on WNUT-17 (social media NER), both metrics yield near-zero signal ($\\rho < 0.20$).\n53\t\\item \\textbf{Cross-model}: On LLaMA-3.1-8B with the same dataset, FK outperforms SJ by 3 points, likely due to format instability confounding structural comparison.\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Cross-task}: On relation extraction, all consistency metrics fail ($\\rho < 0.25$), with error propagation across compound structures (head, tail, type) rendering consistency uninformative.\n55\t\\item \\textbf{Practical utility}: Consistency-based best-of-$N$ selection cannot outperform random selection, despite oracle analysis confirming that better outputs exist in the sample set.\n56\t\\end{itemize}\n57\t\n58\tRather than presenting these as isolated failures, we unify them through three explanatory mechanisms: (1) a \\emph{performance ceiling effect} where high base accuracy compresses the consistency range, (2) a \\emph{capability floor effect} where low accuracy produces ``consistently wrong'' outputs, and (3) \\emph{model-specific confounds} where generation artifacts (boundary wobble, format variation) inject noise orthogonal to true uncertainty.\n59\tThese mechanisms predict an inverted-U relationship between model capability and consistency signal strength, which we confirm through capability stratification analysis.\n60\t\n61\tOur contributions are:\n62\t(1) the first systematic negative result on consistency-based confidence for structured prediction, with controlled experiments isolating each failure mode;\n63\t(2) an error decomposition framework explaining why the consistency--accuracy correlation is fragile;\n64\t(3) practitioner guidelines specifying the narrow operating range where consistency signals may be useful.\n65\t\n66\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n67\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n68\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Self-consistency for reasoning.}\n71\tWang et al.~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} showed that sampling multiple chain-of-thought reasoning paths and taking the majority vote on the final answer substantially improves accuracy on arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning tasks.\n72\tSubsequent work extended this to code generation~\\cite{chen2023codet}, multi-step planning, and tool use.\n73\tThe key insight is that correct reasoning paths tend to converge on the same answer, making agreement a proxy for correctness.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Confidence estimation for LLMs.}\n76\tVerbalized confidence~\\cite{kadavath2022language}, token-level entropy, and ensemble disagreement have been explored as uncertainty estimators.\n77\tFor structured outputs, these approaches face challenges: token entropy does not capture structural coherence, and verbalized confidence requires additional prompting overhead.\n78\tConsistency-based confidence offers an attractive alternative that operates on the output level without access to model internals.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Structured prediction with LLMs.}\n81\tRecent work has shown that LLMs can perform competitive NER and RE when fine-tuned with structured output formats~\\cite{li2023evaluating,wadhwa2023revisiting}.\n82\tHowever, the reliability of these predictions---and methods for estimating it---remains underexplored.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Negative results in NLP.}\n85\tWe position this work in the tradition of empirical negative results that provide diagnostic value~\\cite{rogers2020primer}.\n86\tUnderstanding \\emph{when and why} methods fail is as important as demonstrating success, particularly for practitioners who need to decide whether to deploy consistency-based confidence in production systems.\n87\t\n88\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t\\section{Method}\n90\t\\label{sec:method}\n91\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Task Setup}\n94\t\n95\tWe evaluate on two IE tasks: named entity recognition (NER) and relation extraction (RE).\n96\tFor each test instance $x$, we generate $N$ stochastic samples $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ by decoding from a fine-tuned LLM at temperature $T > 0$.\n97\tEach sample $y_i$ is a structured output: a set of typed entity spans for NER, or a set of (head entity, relation type, tail entity) triplets for RE.\n98\tWe then compute pairwise consistency scores across samples and correlate these with instance-level F1 accuracy.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Consistency Metrics}\n101\t\n102\t\\paragraph{Structural Soft Jaccard (SJ).}\n103\tFor two structured outputs $y_i, y_j$, soft Jaccard computes:\n104\t\\begin{equation}\n105\t\\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \\frac{|y_i \\cap y_j|}{|y_i \\cup y_j|}\n106\t\\end{equation}\n107\twhere intersection and union use soft matching (partial credit for overlapping spans with different boundaries or types).\n108\tThe instance-level SJ score is the mean pairwise SJ across all $\\binom{N}{2}$ sample pairs.\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{Fleiss' Kappa (FK).}\n111\tFK treats the structured output as a flat token sequence and measures inter-rater agreement across $N$ samples at the token level.\n112\tIt accounts for chance agreement but ignores structural relationships between tokens.\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n115\tWe measure the Spearman rank correlation $\\rho$ between instance-level consistency scores (SJ or FK) and instance-level F1 accuracy against gold annotations.\n116\tA useful confidence signal should yield high $\\rho$: instances where samples agree more should be instances where the model is more accurate.\n117\t\n118\t\\subsection{Experimental Configuration}\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n121\t(1) \\textbf{SciERC}~\\cite{luan2018multi}: scientific abstracts, 6 entity types, 7 relation types;\n122\t(2) \\textbf{CoNLL-2003}~\\cite{tjong2003introduction}: news text, 4 entity types;\n123\t(3) \\textbf{WNUT-17}~\\cite{derczynski2017results}: social media text, 6 entity types.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n126\t(1) \\textbf{Qwen3-8B}: fine-tuned with LoRA on each dataset;\n127\t(2) \\textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct}: fine-tuned with LoRA on SciERC.\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Sampling.}\n130\tDefault: $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$.\n131\tWe additionally test $N{=}16$ and $T{=}0.7$.\n132\t\n133\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n134\t\\section{Core Results: Cross-Dataset Fragility}\n135\t\\label{sec:cross_dataset}\n136\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n137\t\n138\t\\subsection{The SJ Advantage Does Not Transfer}\n139\t\n140\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_dataset} presents the central finding: SJ's advantage over FK is confined to a single dataset.\n141\t\n142\t\\begin{table}[t]\n143\t\\centering\n144\t\\small\n145\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n146\t\\toprule\n147\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{Domain} & \\textbf{Types} & \\textbf{F1} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n148\t\\midrule\n149\tSciERC & Scientific & 6 & .667 & .383 & .259 & +.124 \\\\\n150\tCoNLL & News & 4 & .812 & .220 & .240 & $-$.020 \\\\\n151\tWNUT-17 & Social & 6 & .418 & .190 & .150 & +.040 \\\\\n152\t\\bottomrule\n153\t\\end{tabular}\n154\t\\caption{NER consistency--accuracy correlation ($\\rho$, Spearman) across three datasets. $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$ for SciERC; $T{=}0.7$ for CoNLL and WNUT. SJ outperforms FK substantially only on SciERC. On CoNLL, FK is slightly stronger; on WNUT, both are near zero.}\n155\t\\label{tab:cross_dataset}\n156\t\\end{table}\n157\t\n158\tOn SciERC, SJ achieves $\\rho{=}0.383$ (vs.\\ FK $\\rho{=}0.259$, $\\Delta{=}+0.124$), a statistically significant advantage ($p < 10^{-17}$).\n159\tHowever, this result is an outlier:\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{CoNLL-2003: ceiling effect.}\n162\tWith greedy F1 of 0.812, the model is already highly accurate.\n163\tOracle headroom is only 4.4pp (vs.\\ 10.9pp for SciERC), meaning few instances have room for improvement.\n164\tIn this high-accuracy regime, nearly all instances exhibit high consistency regardless of accuracy, compressing the correlation range.\n165\tFK marginally outperforms SJ ($\\rho{=}0.24$ vs.\\ 0.22), likely because CoNLL's simple 4-type output space eliminates the structural complexity that SJ is designed to capture.\n166\t\n167\t\\paragraph{WNUT-17: floor effect.}\n168\tWith greedy F1 of only 0.418 and 38.7\\% of instances receiving zero F1 in at least one sample, the model lacks basic capability on many instances.\n169\tThese ``consistently wrong'' instances contribute high SJ scores (the model generates the same incorrect output repeatedly) that do not correlate with accuracy---a false-confidence phenomenon.\n170\tSJ weakly outperforms FK ($\\Delta{=}+0.04$), but $\\rho{=}0.19$ renders both metrics practically useless as confidence signals.\n171\t\n172\t\\subsection{The Inverted-U Pattern}\n173\t\n174\tThe three datasets reveal an inverted-U relationship between base model performance and consistency signal strength:\n175\ttoo-easy datasets (CoNLL, F1$=$0.81) suffer ceiling effects; too-hard datasets (WNUT, F1$=$0.42) suffer floor effects; only the middle ground (SciERC, F1$=$0.67) provides enough variance for consistency to be informative.\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Out-of-Distribution Collapse}\n178\t\n179\tTo test robustness under distribution shift, we evaluate the WNUT-trained model on out-of-distribution data (Table~\\ref{tab:ood}).\n180\t\n181\t\\begin{table}[t]\n182\t\\centering\n183\t\\small\n184\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n185\t\\toprule\n186\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{F1} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & \\textbf{\\%Zero} & \\textbf{Headroom} \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\tIn-domain & .418 & .190 & .150 & 38.7\\% & 10.4pp \\\\\n189\tOOD & .295 & .120 & .140 & 52.3\\% & 11.3pp \\\\\n190\t\\bottomrule\n191\t\\end{tabular}\n192\t\\caption{Consistency signal under distribution shift (WNUT-17 model). OOD further degrades an already weak signal.}\n193\t\\label{tab:ood}\n194\t\\end{table}\n195\t\n196\tUnder domain shift, greedy F1 drops to 0.295 and over half of instances receive zero F1.\n197\tThe consistency signal collapses further ($\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}{=}0.12$): the model generates diverse but uniformly incorrect outputs, and high consistency no longer indicates accuracy.\n198\tOracle headroom (11.3pp) confirms that better outputs exist in the sample set but are unidentifiable by either metric.\n199\t\n200\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n201\t\\section{Core Results: Temperature Is Not a Rescue}\n202\t\\label{sec:temperature}\n203\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n204\t\n205\tA natural hypothesis is that temperature tuning could improve the consistency signal by controlling the signal-to-noise ratio.\n206\tLower temperature ($T{=}0.7$) should reduce sampling noise, potentially strengthening the consistency--accuracy correlation.\n207\t\n208\t\\begin{table}[t]\n209\t\\centering\n210\t\\small\n211\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n212\t\\toprule\n213\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Metric} & $T{=}0.7$ & $T{=}1.0$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n214\t\\midrule\n215\tNER & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (full) & .376 & .383 & $-$.007 \\\\\n216\tNER & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ (full) & .268 & .259 & +.009 \\\\\n217\tNER & Oracle headroom & 7.2pp & 10.9pp & $-$3.7pp \\\\\n218\tNER & \\%Zero F1 & 9.8\\% & 12.2\\% & $-$2.4pp \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tRE & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (full) & .250 & .244 & +.006 \\\\\n221\tRE & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (cond) & .280 & .316 & $-$.036 \\\\\n222\tRE & Oracle headroom & 11.2pp & 16.7pp & $-$5.5pp \\\\\n223\tRE & \\%Zero F1 & 41.2\\% & 44.3\\% & $-$3.1pp \\\\\n224\t\\bottomrule\n225\t\\end{tabular}\n226\t\\caption{Temperature sensitivity on SciERC ($N{=}8$). Lowering temperature to 0.7 does not systematically improve consistency signal quality, while substantially reducing oracle headroom.}\n227\t\\label{tab:temperature}\n228\t\\end{table}\n229\t\n230\tTable~\\ref{tab:temperature} shows that temperature is not an effective tuning knob.\n231\tFor NER, $T{=}0.7$ leaves $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ essentially unchanged (0.376 vs.\\ 0.383) while reducing oracle headroom by 3.7pp---the samples become more similar but not more informatively similar.\n232\tFor RE, the effect is mixed: full-set $\\rho$ is unchanged, but conditional $\\rho$ \\emph{decreases} from 0.316 to 0.280.\n233\tThe mechanism is that lower temperature increases the proportion of all-zero instances (more instances where the model generates empty or identical outputs), and after filtering these, the remaining conditional set is more homogeneous, reducing SJ's discriminative power.\n234\t\n235\tThe fundamental issue is that temperature controls output \\emph{diversity} but not the \\emph{informativeness} of that diversity.\n236\tReducing temperature suppresses both noise (unhelpful variation) and signal (meaningful variation that correlates with uncertainty) in roughly equal measure.\n237\t\n238\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n239\t\\section{Core Results: Capability Stratification}\n240\t\\label{sec:stratification}\n241\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n242\t\n243\tTo directly test the inverted-U hypothesis from Section~\\ref{sec:cross_dataset}, we stratify instances within SciERC by greedy F1 and compute $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ per stratum.\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[t]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\small\n248\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n249\t\\toprule\n250\t\\textbf{F1 Stratum} & \\textbf{NER $n$} & \\textbf{NER $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$} & \\textbf{RE $n$} & \\textbf{RE $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$} \\\\\n251\t\\midrule\n252\t$[0, 0.2)$ & 68 & .08 & 157 & .05 \\\\\n253\t$[0.2, 0.4)$ & 72 & .14 & 83 & .11 \\\\\n254\t$[0.4, 0.6)$ & 89 & .22 & 62 & .18 \\\\\n255\t$[0.6, 0.8)$ & 118 & .31 & 55 & .15 \\\\\n256\t$[0.8, 1.0]$ & 182 & .12 & 40 & .07 \\\\\n257\t\\bottomrule\n258\t\\end{tabular}\n259\t\\caption{$\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ by instance difficulty stratum on SciERC ($N{=}8$). Both tasks show an inverted-U pattern, but monotonic trend tests are not significant (NER $p{=}0.12$; RE $p{=}0.38$).}\n260\t\\label{tab:stratification}\n261\t\\end{table}\n262\t\n263\tTable~\\ref{tab:stratification} confirms the qualitative pattern: consistency signal peaks in the mid-difficulty range ([0.6, 0.8) for NER, [0.4, 0.6) for RE) and drops at both extremes.\n264\tAt the low end ($F1 < 0.2$), the model consistently fails---consistency is high but uninformative.\n265\tAt the high end ($F1 > 0.8$), the model consistently succeeds---consistency is again uniformly high, providing no discriminative signal.\n266\t\n267\tHowever, the pattern is not statistically robust: Jonckheere-Terpstra monotonic trend tests yield $p{=}0.12$ for NER and $p{=}0.38$ for RE, reflecting the small per-stratum sample sizes (40--182 instances).\n268\tThis means the ``Goldilocks zone'' for consistency-based confidence is suggestive but cannot be precisely delineated from our data.\n269\t\n270\tFor practitioners, the qualitative implication is clear: before deploying consistency-based confidence, evaluate the model's greedy performance.\n271\tIf F1 $< 0.50$ or F1 $> 0.80$, the consistency signal is likely uninformative.\n272\t\n273\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n274\t\\section{Analysis: Why Does Consistency Fail?}\n275\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n276\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n277\t\n278\t\\subsection{Sampling Beyond Saturation}\n279\t\n280\tIf consistency-based confidence were a reliable signal, increasing the sampling budget $N$ should improve estimation quality.\n281\tTable~\\ref{tab:nscaling} tests this on SciERC.\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[t]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n287\t\\toprule\n288\t$N$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ & Headroom & \\%Zero \\\\\n289\t\\midrule\n290\t\\multicolumn{6}{c}{\\emph{NER}} \\\\\n291\t8 & .383 & .259 & +.124 & 10.9pp & 12.2\\% \\\\\n292\t16 & .362 & .278 & +.084 & 7.5pp & 11.5\\% \\\\\n293\t$\\delta$ & $-$.021 & +.019 & $-$.040 & $-$3.4pp & $-$0.7pp \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\t\\multicolumn{6}{c}{\\emph{RE}} \\\\\n296\t8 & .244 & .278 & $-$.034 & 16.7pp & 44.3\\% \\\\\n297\t16 & .231 & .268 & $-$.037 & 13.7pp & 42.8\\% \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\caption{Effect of doubling sample budget ($N{=}8 \\to 16$) on SciERC. SJ correlation decreases for both tasks; additional samples introduce noise without improving signal quality.}\n301\t\\label{tab:nscaling}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\tDoubling the sample budget from 8 to 16 does not improve the consistency signal---for NER, $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ \\emph{decreases} from 0.383 to 0.362 (cross-seed mean, std$=$0.009, Table~\\ref{tab:crossseed}).\n305\tThe mechanism: at $T{=}1.0$, additional samples increasingly trigger low-probability tokens, generating spurious outputs (hallucinated entities, missed spans) that reduce mean SJ without corresponding to genuine accuracy differences.\n306\tOracle headroom also decreases (10.9pp $\\to$ 7.5pp), confirming that the additional samples do not discover meaningfully better outputs.\n307\t\n308\t\\begin{table}[t]\n309\t\\centering\n310\t\\small\n311\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n312\t\\toprule\n313\t\\textbf{Seed} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (full) & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ (full) & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ (cond) \\\\\n314\t\\midrule\n315\t42 & .371 & .282 & .365 \\\\\n316\t123 & .354 & .270 & .348 \\\\\n317\t456 & .362 & .281 & .361 \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tMean$\\pm$Std & .362$\\pm$.009 & .278$\\pm$.007 & .358$\\pm$.009 \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\caption{Cross-seed stability of $\\rho$ at $N{=}16$ (NER, SciERC). The degradation from $N{=}8$ is consistent across seeds (std $=$ 0.009).}\n323\t\\label{tab:crossseed}\n324\t\\end{table}\n325\t\n326\tThis directly undermines the test-time scaling premise: if more compute (more samples) does not yield better confidence estimates, the compute--accuracy tradeoff collapses.\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Selection Curves: Consistency Cannot Beat Random}\n329\t\n330\t\\begin{table}[t]\n331\t\\centering\n332\t\\small\n333\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n334\t\\toprule\n335\t$N$ & Oracle & SJ & FK & Random \\\\\n336\t\\midrule\n337\t1 & .667 & .667 & .667 & .667 \\\\\n338\t2 & .698 & .670 & .671 & .671 \\\\\n339\t4 & .719 & .674 & .676 & .675 \\\\\n340\t8 & .742 & .677 & .680 & .679 \\\\\n341\t16 & .742 & .679 & .684 & .681 \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 on SciERC NER. SJ-based selection tracks random selection; the oracle--method gap widens with $N$, indicating that better outputs exist but cannot be identified by consistency.}\n345\t\\label{tab:selection}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\tTable~\\ref{tab:selection} translates the abstract finding ``$\\rho$ is low'' into a concrete practical statement.\n349\tSJ-based best-of-$N$ selection achieves F1$=$0.679 at $N{=}16$---\\emph{below} random selection (0.681, $p{=}0.47$).\n350\tFK performs marginally better (0.684) but is also statistically indistinguishable from random ($p{=}0.58$).\n351\tMeanwhile, oracle selection achieves 0.742, confirming a 7.5pp gap of unexploitable headroom.\n352\t\n353\tThe failure is fundamental: with $\\rho \\approx 0.36$, consistency explains only $\\sim$13\\% of the variance in instance quality ($R^2 \\approx 0.13$).\n354\tAt this signal-to-noise ratio, the order-statistics boost from random best-of-$N$ selection (which increases in expectation even without ranking information) matches or exceeds any advantage from consistency-guided selection.\n355\t\n356\t\\subsection{Error Propagation in Relational Tasks}\n357\t\n358\t\\begin{table}[t]\n359\t\\centering\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n362\t\\toprule\n363\t\\textbf{Relation Type} & $n$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\tUsed-for & 156 & .28 & .26 & +.02 \\\\\n366\tFeature-of & 72 & .21 & .24 & $-$.03 \\\\\n367\tPart-of & 58 & .19 & .22 & $-$.03 \\\\\n368\tEvaluate-for & 43 & .16 & .18 & $-$.02 \\\\\n369\tHyponym-of & 34 & .14 & .19 & $-$.05 \\\\\n370\tConjunction & 21 & .11 & .08 & +.03 \\\\\n371\tCompare & 13 & .09 & .12 & $-$.03 \\\\\n372\t\\bottomrule\n373\t\\end{tabular}\n374\t\\caption{Per-relation-type $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ on SciERC RE ($N{=}8$). All types exhibit weak correlation. The bootstrap 95\\% CI for the overall SJ$-$FK difference spans zero: [$-$0.08, +0.05].}\n375\t\\label{tab:pertype}\n376\t\\end{table}\n377\t\n378\tA potential explanation for RE's weak consistency signal is that specific hard relation types drag down the average.\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:pertype} rules this out: \\emph{all} relation types show weak $\\rho$ (range: 0.09--0.28), and the SJ--FK difference is inconsistent in direction across types.\n380\tThe bootstrap 95\\% CI for the aggregate SJ$-$FK difference spans zero ([$-$0.08, +0.05]), meaning we cannot even establish whether SJ is better than FK for RE.\n381\t\n382\tThe failure is structural rather than type-specific.\n383\tEach relation triplet (head, tail, type) has three independently varying components; a single component error (e.g., wrong tail boundary) can produce a completely non-matching triplet, amplifying noise in both SJ and FK scores.\n384\tThis \\emph{compound error propagation} is absent in NER, where each entity is evaluated independently.\n385\t\n386\tRE at $N{=}16$ (Table~\\ref{tab:re_n16}) confirms the negative finding with higher statistical power: $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}{=}0.231$ (bootstrap 95\\% CI: [0.18, 0.28]).\n387\t\n388\t\\begin{table}[t]\n389\t\\centering\n390\t\\small\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\t$N$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}^{c}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}^{c}$ & Headroom \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\t8 & .244 & .278 & .316 & .307 & 16.7pp \\\\\n396\t16 & .231 & .268 & .270 & .290 & 13.7pp \\\\\n397\t\\bottomrule\n398\t\\end{tabular}\n399\t\\caption{RE $N$-scaling on SciERC. Superscript $c$ denotes conditional (non-zero instances only). Signal degrades with more samples.}\n400\t\\label{tab:re_n16}\n401\t\\end{table}\n402\t\n403\t\\subsection{Model-Specific Confounds}\n404\t\n405\t\\begin{table}[t]\n406\t\\centering\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{F1} & $\\rho_{\\text{SJ}}$ & $\\rho_{\\text{FK}}$ & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{Parse Fail} \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tQwen3-8B & .667 & .383 & .259 & +.124 & 0 \\\\\n413\tLLaMA-3.1-8B & .591 & .280 & .310 & $-$.030 & 12 \\\\\n414\t\\bottomrule\n415\t\\end{tabular}\n416\t\\caption{Model comparison on SciERC NER ($N{=}8$). SJ's advantage vanishes for LLaMA, where format instability and boundary wobble confound structural comparison.}\n417\t\\label{tab:multimodel}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tTable~\\ref{tab:multimodel} tests whether SJ's advantage is model-agnostic.\n421\tOn LLaMA-3.1-8B with the same dataset (SciERC NER), FK outperforms SJ ($\\rho{=}0.31$ vs.\\ 0.28), reversing the Qwen3-8B result.\n422\tTwo factors contribute:\n423\t\n424\t\\paragraph{Format instability.}\n425\tLLaMA produces 12 parse failures across stochastic samples (vs.\\ 0 for Qwen3-8B).\n426\tSJ interprets format inconsistency as low confidence, but these failures reflect generation style differences, not genuine uncertainty.\n427\t\n428\t\\paragraph{Boundary wobble.}\n429\tLLaMA's entity span boundaries exhibit more variation across samples (wider logit distribution at boundary tokens).\n430\tSJ's soft matching partially credits boundary-shifted spans, but this variation is noise---it does not correlate with accuracy.\n431\tFK's binary token-level agreement is less sensitive to boundary shifts, giving it an advantage.\n432\t\n433\tThese model-specific confounds mean that deploying SJ requires validation not just per dataset but per model, severely limiting its practical utility.\n434\t\n435\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n436\t\\section{Discussion}\n437\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n438\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n439\t\n440\t\\subsection{Practitioner Guidelines}\n441\t\n442\tOur results suggest the following conditions must \\emph{all} hold for consistency-based confidence to be potentially useful for structured prediction:\n443\t\n444\t\\begin{enumerate}\n445\t\\item \\textbf{Mid-range base performance}: Greedy F1 should be in approximately [0.50, 0.80]. Below 0.50, floor effects dominate; above 0.80, ceiling effects compress the signal.\n446\t\\item \\textbf{Simple output structure}: NER-like tasks where errors are independent per entity. Compound structures (RE triplets) introduce error propagation that destroys the consistency--accuracy link.\n447\t\\item \\textbf{Stable generation format}: The model must produce well-formed outputs consistently. Format variation and boundary wobble confound structural metrics.\n448\t\\item \\textbf{In-distribution data}: Distribution shift causes the consistency signal to collapse ($\\rho{=}0.12$ OOD vs.\\ 0.19 in-domain).\n449\t\\end{enumerate}\n450\t\n451\tThese conditions are restrictive. In practice, the scenarios where consistency is most \\emph{needed} (hard instances, complex tasks, OOD data) are precisely the scenarios where it fails.\n452\t\n453\t\\subsection{Why Structured Prediction Is Different from Reasoning}\n454\t\n455\tSelf-consistency succeeds for reasoning tasks because: (a) the output space is discrete and small (a number, a multiple-choice letter), making majority voting straightforward; (b) correct reasoning paths converge deterministically on the correct answer; (c) errors are typically ``randomly wrong'' rather than ``consistently wrong.''\n456\t\n457\tStructured prediction violates all three properties: (a) the output space is combinatorial (exponential in the number of entities/relations); (b) multiple structurally different outputs can be partially correct; (c) systematic model weaknesses produce consistent errors (e.g., always missing a specific entity type).\n458\t\n459\t\\subsection{Limitations}\n460\t\n461\tOur study has several limitations.\n462\tFirst, we evaluate only 8B-parameter models; larger models with stronger instruction-following may exhibit different sampling behavior.\n463\tSecond, our SJ metric uses a specific soft matching formulation; alternative structural similarity metrics (e.g., graph edit distance for RE) might perform differently.\n464\tThird, we evaluate on English IE datasets only; cross-lingual generalization is unknown.\n465\tFourth, the capability stratification analysis (Section~\\ref{sec:stratification}) lacks statistical power due to small per-stratum sample sizes.\n466\t\n467\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n468\t\\section{Conclusion}\n469\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n470\t\n471\tWe presented a systematic evaluation of self-consistency as a confidence signal for structured information extraction.\n472\tOur findings are predominantly negative: the consistency--accuracy correlation is fragile, dataset-specific, model-specific, and does not enable practical test-time scaling through best-of-$N$ selection.\n473\tThrough error decomposition and capability stratification, we identified three failure modes---performance ceiling effects, capability floor effects, and model-specific sampling confounds---that together predict a narrow operating range for consistency-based confidence.\n474\tWe hope these results help practitioners make informed decisions about when (not) to invest in consistency-based confidence estimation, and motivate future work on more robust confidence signals for structured prediction.\n475\t\n476\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n477\t\n478\t\\bibitem{wang2023selfconsistency}\n479\tXuezhi Wang, Jason Wei, Dale Schuurmans, Quoc Le, Ed Chi, Sharan Narang, Aakanksha Chowdhery, and Denny Zhou.\n480\tSelf-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models.\n481\tIn \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n482\t\n483\t\\bibitem{brown2024large}\n484\tBradley Brown, Jordan Juravsky, Ryan Ehrlich, Ronald Clark, Quoc V. Le, Christopher R\\'{e}, and Azalia Mirhoseini.\n485\tLarge language monkeys: Scaling inference compute with repeated sampling.\n486\t\\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.21787}, 2024.\n487\t\n488\t\\bibitem{chen2023codet}\n489\tBei Chen, Fengji Zhang, Anh Nguyen, Daoguang Zan, Zhiruo Lin, Jian-Guang Lou, and Weizhu Chen.\n490\tCodeT: Code generation with generated tests.\n491\tIn \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n492\t\n493\t\\bibitem{kadavath2022language}\n494\tSaurav Kadavath, Tom Conerly, Amanda Askell, et al.\n495\tLanguage models (mostly) know what they know.\n496\t\\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2207.05221}, 2022.\n497\t\n498\t\\bibitem{li2023evaluating}\n499\tPeng Li, Tianxiang Sun, Qiong Tang, Hang Yan, Yuanbin Wu, Xuanjing Huang, and Xipeng Qiu.\n500\tEvaluating ChatGPT's information extraction capabilities.\n501\t\\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.11633}, 2023.\n502\t\n503\t\\bibitem{wadhwa2023revisiting}\n504\tSomin Wadhwa, Silvio Amir, and Byron C. Wallace.\n505\tRevisiting relation extraction in the era of large language models.\n506\tIn \\emph{ACL}, 2023.\n507\t\n508\t\\bibitem{rogers2020primer}\n509\tAnna Rogers.\n510\tA primer on negative results in NLP.\n511\tIn \\emph{ACL}, 2020.\n512\t\n513\t\\bibitem{luan2018multi}\n514\tYi Luan, Luheng He, Mari Ostendorf, and Hannaneh Hajishirzi.\n515\tMulti-task identification of entities, relations, and coreference for scientific knowledge graph construction.\n516\tIn \\emph{EMNLP}, 2018.\n517\t\n518\t\\bibitem{tjong2003introduction}\n519\tErik F. Tjong Kim Sang and Fien De Meulder.\n520\tIntroduction to the {CoNLL}-2003 shared task: Language-independent named entity recognition.\n521\tIn \\emph{CoNLL}, 2003.\n522\t\n523\t\\bibitem{derczynski2017results}\n524\tLeon Derczynski, Eric Nichols, Marieke van Erp, and Nut Limsopatham.\n525\tResults of the {WNUT}2017 shared task on novel and emerging entity recognition.\n526\tIn \\emph{W-NUT}, 2017.\n527\t\n528\t\\end{thebibliography}\n529\t\n530\t\\end{document}\n531\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKfzCDwhxMENhz8xdBgfGN","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"b3f4ad5b-51c6-4a2c-ab71-13e20f857303","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:23:24.897Z"}
