{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Vd2BmrQoh32qeuy8jDwFVQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ApaU9oBUaWr15xJKcvJfwD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/median/review_report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16285,"cache_read_input_tokens":62164,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16285,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"62afb81c-8e76-48a1-af0e-0c8b53c0d33f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ApaU9oBUaWr15xJKcvJfwD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T093336\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: borderline reject (3/6)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T10:21:07.178565+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report — Consolidated Roundtable\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** \"When Does Structural Self-Consistency Work? An Empirical Study on Information Extraction Test-Time Scaling\"\n11\t**Dream ID:** 20260514T093336\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n13\t**Overall Score:** 3/6 — Borderline Reject\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Roundtable Summary\n18\t\n19\t| Reviewer | Focus | Score | Key Concern |\n20\t|----------|-------|-------|-------------|\n21\t| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 3/6 | Weak baseline (FK only), insufficient evaluation metrics (ρ only), type-granularity trend on 3 confounded points |\n22\t| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 3/6 | Thin novelty (metric substitution), missing critical baselines (log-prob, USC), formula-implementation discrepancy |\n23\t| **Consensus** | — | **3/6** | Both reviewers converge on the same score independently. No arbitration needed. |\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## Paper Summary\n28\t\n29\tThe paper proposes structural self-consistency — operationalized as the soft Jaccard coefficient (SJ) over sampled structured outputs — as a confidence signal for information extraction test-time scaling. SJ is compared against Fleiss' Kappa (FK, a surface-agreement baseline) via Spearman rank correlation with instance-level F1. Experiments span 3 NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes), SciERC relation extraction, and 2 model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B). The core findings: SJ outperforms FK for NER (ρ=0.41 vs 0.29 on SciERC, gap moderated by type granularity); compound error propagation degrades RE signal on the full set (ρ=0.26) but conditional analysis recovers ρ=0.33; an inverted-U pattern shows SJ is most informative for medium-difficulty instances; SJ-based selection yields +1.8pp over greedy (14.8% of oracle headroom).\n30\t\n31\t---\n32\t\n33\t## Consolidated Strengths\n34\t\n35\t**S1. Comprehensive experimental coverage.** (R1, R2 agree) All 11 planned experiments are reported. The breadth — N-scaling, cross-dataset, cross-model, temperature sensitivity, capability stratification, per-relation-type diagnostic, adaptive compute, selection curves, OOD robustness — substantially exceeds typical empirical studies.\n36\t\n37\t**S2. The inverted-U diagnostic finding is genuinely insightful.** (R1, R2 agree) The observation that consistency metrics are most informative for medium-difficulty instances — where the model exhibits genuine uncertainty rather than consistent success or failure — provides a principled, generalizable explanation for why overall ρ is moderate. The conceptual separation between \"uncertainty\" and \"inability\" is a real intellectual contribution.\n38\t\n39\t**S3. Honest treatment of negative results.** (R1, R2 agree) The paper does not hide the RE failure, the OntoNotes weakness, or the modest adaptive compute gains. The \"when does it work\" framing is scientifically mature. The progressive narrowing of claims (SciERC strong → CoNLL moderate → OntoNotes weak → RE fails) is intellectually honest and builds trust.\n40\t\n41\t**S4. Statistical rigor on primary comparisons.** Permutation tests, bootstrap CIs for stratification, p < 10^{-22} for the main NER finding, and reporting both full and conditional sets are good practices.\n42\t\n43\t**S5. Well-motivated problem.** Extending self-consistency from atomic reasoning outputs to structured prediction is a natural and timely research question. The observation that surface agreement misses structural similarity is correct.\n44\t\n45\t---\n46\t\n47\t## Consolidated Weaknesses\n48\t\n49\t### Critical (both reviewers flag independently)\n50\t\n51\t**W1. (CRITICAL) Missing stronger baselines — the paper's most damaging gap.** Both reviewers independently identified this as the single biggest problem. FK is a weak, arguably strawman baseline — it operates on token-level BIO labels and was never designed for set-structured outputs. The paper's central claim (\"structural consistency outperforms surface agreement\") is only validated against this one weak metric. Missing baselines that are necessary to calibrate the contribution:\n52\t\n53\t- **Sequence log-probability / token-level entropy**: The cheapest confidence signal (zero additional inference). If SJ doesn't beat log-prob, the 16× inference cost is unjustified.\n54\t- **Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al., NAACL 2024)**: Extends self-consistency to non-votable structured outputs using LLM-as-judge. The single most relevant prior work — entirely absent.\n55\t- **Exact-match voting rate**: The direct analogue of Wang et al.'s original self-consistency for structured outputs.\n56\t- **Entity-level majority voting**: A natural structured consistency baseline using the same N samples but selecting per-entity rather than per-output.\n57\t- **Semantic uncertainty / clustering methods** (Kuhn et al., ICML 2023).\n58\t\n59\tWithout these, we cannot determine whether SJ's advantage reflects genuine merit or just FK's inadequacy.\n60\t\n61\t**W2. (CRITICAL) Thin novelty — metric substitution without theoretical grounding.** (R2 primary, R1 concurs implicitly) The technical contribution reduces to applying an existing similarity metric (soft Jaccard with bipartite matching) in an existing framework (sample-and-select). The \"structural\" label overstates what SJ captures: it operates on flat sets of (span, type) tuples without modeling inter-entity dependencies, schema constraints, or graph-level properties. No theoretical analysis is provided for why SJ should correlate with accuracy, or under what formal conditions the correlation holds. The metric definition (Eq. 1–3) occupies less than half a page with no algorithmic novelty.\n62\t\n63\t### Major\n64\t\n65\t**W3. (Major) Evaluation protocol relies solely on Spearman ρ.** (R1) A confidence signal's utility extends beyond rank correlation: AUROC (can the metric distinguish good vs. bad outputs at a threshold?), calibration (is SJ=0.8 associated with ~80% accuracy?), and Kendall's τ (robustness to tied ranks, which are common with small entity sets). Reporting only ρ leaves open the possibility that SJ has better rank correlation but worse calibration than alternatives.\n66\t\n67\t**W4. (Major) Formula-implementation discrepancy.** (R2) The paper's Equation 1 defines SJ via symmetric max-match with max(|E_i|,|E_j|) denominator, while the actual implementation uses type-grouped Hungarian (one-to-one) matching with max(|g_a|,|g_b|) per type group. These are meaningfully different algorithms. Anyone attempting to reproduce from the paper equations would obtain different results. This must be reconciled.\n68\t\n69\t**W5. (Major) Type-granularity trend rests on 3 confounded data points.** (R1) The claimed trend (4→6→18 types maps to SJ-FK gap of +7pp→+12pp→+3pp) is: (a) only 3 data points; (b) non-monotonic (CoNLL with 4 types has smaller gap than SciERC with 6); (c) confounded by domain, entity density, annotation quality, baseline performance, and test set size. The paper acknowledges this but then uses the trend as a central organizing principle, overstating the evidence.\n70\t\n71\t**W6. (Major) Inconsistent N across the cross-dataset table.** (R2) Table 2 compares SciERC at N=16 against CoNLL and OntoNotes at N=8. Table 1 shows N=8→N=16 improves ρ_SJ by +0.027 on SciERC. At consistent N=8, SciERC's ρ_SJ=0.383 — actually comparable to CoNLL's 0.37. The current presentation inflates SciERC's apparent advantage.\n72\t\n73\t### Moderate\n74\t\n75\t**W7. (Moderate) Conditional set threshold is ad hoc with no sensitivity analysis.** (R1) The all-zero exclusion is a binary heuristic. The paper does not report how conditional ρ varies with softer thresholds (e.g., exclude where >75% samples are F1=0, or max sample F1 < 0.1). This sensitivity analysis is critical for the RE conditional claim (ρ=0.33).\n76\t\n77\t**W8. (Moderate) Only 2 models at 8B scale.** (R1, R2) The SJ-FK gap narrows from +12pp (Qwen) to +7pp (LLaMA), raising the question of whether the gap vanishes for stronger or larger models where surface agreement may suffice. No 70B+ or API-based models tested.\n78\t\n79\t**W9. (Moderate) Adaptive compute gain is underwhelming.** (R1) +1.8pp over greedy at 16× inference cost, demonstrated only on SciERC NER. Only +0.8pp over FK selection. 14.8% of oracle headroom captured. No adaptive compute results for other datasets or RE.\n80\t\n81\t**W10. (Moderate) Single RE dataset limits diagnostic claims.** (R2) All RE analysis relies on SciERC (397 instances). The compound error propagation explanation is plausible but dataset-specific. A second RE dataset (TACRED, DocRED) would strengthen Claim 2.\n82\t\n83\t**W11. (Moderate) Evaluation circularity.** (R2) SJ is computed from T=0.7 samples but evaluated against greedy (T=0) F1. Best-of-N selection operates on the T=0.7 samples themselves. A more direct evaluation would correlate leave-one-out SJ with each sample's own F1.\n84\t\n85\t### Minor\n86\t\n87\t**W12. (Minor) Reference list is thin.** Only ~16 references — far below the 30–40 typical for a top venue.\n88\t\n89\t**W13. (Minor) Figure 11 described textually rather than shown.** Selection curves are important for TTS papers; the line plot would be more informative than Table 11.\n90\t\n91\t**W14. (Minor) No computational overhead analysis for SJ.** Bipartite matching has O(n³) complexity per pair; with N(N-1)/2 pairs this adds non-trivial cost that should be reported.\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## Missing Related Work (R2)\n96\t\n97\t1. **Universal Self-Consistency** (Chen et al., NAACL 2024) — the most directly relevant prior work, entirely absent\n98\t2. **Semantic Uncertainty** (Kuhn et al., ICML 2023) — clustering-based uncertainty for free-form outputs\n99\t3. **LLM calibration** (Kadavath et al. 2022, Xiong et al. 2024) — log-probability and verbalized confidence baselines\n100\t4. **SelfCheckGPT** (Manakul et al. 2023) — cited but not adequately distinguished\n101\t5. **Agreement-based active learning** (Settles 2009, Houlsby et al. 2011) — inter-sample agreement as uncertainty signal\n102\t6. **Structured prediction calibration** (Kuleshov & Liang 2015, Desai & Durrett 2020)\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n107\t\n108\tAll 11 planned experiments appear in the paper — full coverage. However, the results match the expected values from the design document with suspicious precision (e.g., ρ_SJ=0.41 expected → 0.41 obtained; ρ_SJ=0.37 expected → 0.37 obtained across all experiments). While this could reflect accurate planning, it reduces confidence in the experiments being genuinely empirical rather than reverse-engineered.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## Key Questions for Authors\n113\t\n114\t**Q1.** How does SJ compare against sequence log-probability as a selection signal? This is the cheapest baseline and the most natural comparator.\n115\t\n116\t**Q2.** Are you aware of Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024)? How does your approach compare?\n117\t\n118\t**Q3.** Can you reconcile the paper's Equation 1 (symmetric max-match) with the implementation (type-grouped Hungarian matching)?\n119\t\n120\t**Q4.** What are the cross-dataset ρ values at consistent N=8 for all three NER datasets?\n121\t\n122\t**Q5.** How does conditional ρ change with softer exclusion thresholds for the RE analysis?\n123\t\n124\t**Q6.** How does SJ handle the empty-empty case (both samples produce zero entities)? This matters for RE where 42–51% of samples are F1=0.\n125\t\n126\t**Q7.** Why is adaptive compute evaluated only on SciERC NER? Results on CoNLL and OntoNotes would substantially strengthen the practical utility claim.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## Scores\n131\t\n132\t| Dimension | Score | Notes |\n133\t|-----------|-------|-------|\n134\t| Novelty | 2/6 | Metric substitution without theoretical grounding; \"structural\" label overstates contribution |\n135\t| Methodology | 3/6 | Reasonable formulation but unjustified design choices, single weak baseline, ρ-only evaluation |\n136\t| Experiments | 4/6 | Comprehensive breadth, but inconsistent conditions (mixed N), missing baselines, single-metric evaluation |\n137\t| Significance | 2.5/6 | Moderate correlations, underwhelming adaptive compute gains, many caveats on applicability |\n138\t| Clarity | 4/6 | Well-written, honest framing, but formula discrepancy and inconsistent table conditions are concerning |\n139\t| **Overall** | **3/6** | **Borderline Reject** |\n140\t\n141\t---\n142\t\n143\t## Meta-Assessment\n144\t\n145\t**What the paper does well:** The diagnostic contribution — identifying when and why structural consistency works (inverted-U, compound error, type granularity moderation) — is the paper's genuine strength. The experimental breadth is impressive, and the honest treatment of negative results builds trust.\n146\t\n147\t**Why it falls short of the acceptance bar (~25%):** The methodological contribution is too thin (metric substitution), the evaluation framework is too narrow (one weak baseline, one evaluation metric), and the practical impact is too modest (+1.8pp at 16× cost, 14.8% headroom capture). The formula-implementation discrepancy is an additional red flag.\n148\t\n149\t**Path to acceptance:** The clearest path is:\n150\t1. Add 2–3 stronger baselines (log-probability, USC, entity-level voting). If SJ beats log-prob, the story becomes compelling.\n151\t2. Report AUROC and calibration metrics alongside ρ.\n152\t3. Fix the formula-implementation discrepancy.\n153\t4. Standardize N across Table 2.\n154\t5. Add threshold sensitivity analysis for conditional RE.\n155\t6. Expand references to 30+.\n156\t7. Reframe contribution honestly as \"set-aware consistency scoring with diagnostic analysis of boundary conditions.\"\n157\t\n158\t**Realistic venue target:** EMNLP Findings with the above revisions. Main conference would additionally require stronger baselines showing SJ beats log-probability and demonstration of practical utility across multiple datasets.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"1384c5c2-0eef-441a-a4bf-334bf69e9d0b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:23:43.098Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2059b4ae02bc538c","tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":101994,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":192533},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"a36b55bf-59ad-4a51-b50d-b7d339c4f5d0","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012dBRTLvvkTm6rDnZVVkife","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TEayNcK1VGbGEq2AzV9Da9","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260514T093336","overall_score":"reject (2/6)","report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T093336\ntier: worst\noverall_score: reject (2/6)\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14T10:30:00+00:00\n---\n\n# Review Report — Reviewer R2 (Novelty, Significance, Positioning)\n\n**Paper:** \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction\"\n**Tier:** Worst\n**Dream ID:** dream_20260514T093336\n**Review Focus:** Novelty/significance, related work coverage, positioning\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n### 1.1 Research Question\n\nThe research question — whether consistency-based confidence signals transfer from reasoning to structured prediction — is a reasonable one, but it is not particularly novel. The observation that majority voting breaks down when outputs are not atomic is well-known in the ensemble methods and uncertainty quantification literature. The specific application to IE with soft Jaccard is new in implementation but not in concept: set-overlap-based agreement is a standard idea in evaluation metrics (SacreBLEU, ROUGE, entity-level F1 with partial matching), and repurposing evaluation metrics as confidence scores is a long-standing approach.\n\nMore importantly, Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024) directly addresses extending self-consistency to non-votable outputs, which is exactly the motivating problem here. The paper does not cite or engage with this work at all.\n\n**Verdict:** The research question has moderate novelty, diminished by the existence of USC and the general predictability of the outcome.\n\n### 1.2 Failure Modes\n\nThe three failure modes identified — ceiling effect, floor effect, model-specific confounds — are **predictable from first principles** rather than novel discoveries:\n\n- **Ceiling effect:** If a model already achieves 0.81 F1, variance in both consistency and accuracy is compressed. Any rank correlation will be attenuated by range restriction. This is a basic statistical phenomenon (restriction of range), not a finding about self-consistency.\n- **Floor effect:** If a model fails on 39% of instances (WNUT-17), the \"consistently wrong\" pattern is the expected behavior of any agreement metric applied to a model operating below its capability frontier. This has been extensively studied in the calibration literature under the term \"overconfidence in low-accuracy regimes.\"\n- **Model-specific confounds:** That different LLMs exhibit different sampling behaviors (format stability, boundary precision) is empirically obvious and widely known. The observation that LLaMA-3.1-8B has more parse failures (12 vs 0 for Qwen3-8B) is a specific data point but not a conceptual advance.\n\nNone of these failure modes require the specific experimental apparatus here to identify — they can be predicted from understanding range restriction (ceiling), base-rate effects (floor), and model-specific generation artifacts (confounds).\n\n### 1.3 Inverted-U Pattern\n\nThe inverted-U pattern is the paper's most interesting finding, but it suffers from two problems: (a) it is demonstrated across only 3 data points at the cross-dataset level (CoNLL=0.81 ceiling, SciERC=0.67 sweet spot, WNUT=0.42 floor), which is insufficient to establish any functional form; (b) the within-dataset stratification analysis (Table 6) is not statistically significant (NER p=0.12, RE p=0.38). The paper is honest about the latter limitation, but the pattern remains suggestive rather than established. An inverted-U between difficulty and informativeness of agreement measures is, moreover, a well-known phenomenon in the calibration literature — this paper rediscovers it in a specific context without connecting to the broader theoretical framework.\n\n### 1.4 Comparison to Known Findings\n\nThe finding that consistency-based metrics are unreliable for complex structured outputs echoes several established results: (a) Kuhn et al. (ICML 2023) showed that surface-level agreement is a poor uncertainty estimator for free-form generation, motivating semantic clustering; (b) the literature on epistemic vs. aleatoric uncertainty has long noted that agreement measures conflate the two, which is exactly what the \"uncertainty vs. inability\" distinction here recapitulates; (c) selective prediction and conformal prediction literature has established that sample-based confidence requires calibration to be useful, which the paper does not attempt.\n\n**Overall novelty:** Low. The paper applies an obvious metric to an obvious question, obtains the predictable answer (it mostly doesn't work), and the explanatory framework rediscovers known statistical phenomena without connecting to prior theory.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n### 2.1 Quantitative Assessment\n\nThe paper contains **10 references**. For a top venue targeting ~25% acceptance, this is critically insufficient. Comparable empirical analysis papers at EMNLP/ACL typically contain 30-50 references. The related work section is approximately half a page with four short paragraphs. This is the shortest related work section I have seen in a candidate for a top venue.\n\n### 2.2 Critical Missing References\n\nThe following omissions are significant enough to undermine the paper's positioning:\n\n**Self-consistency extensions (directly relevant):**\n- Chen et al., \"Universal Self-Consistency for Large Language Model Generation,\" NAACL 2024. This paper extends self-consistency to non-votable outputs using LLM-as-judge — the exact motivating problem. Its absence is a major gap.\n- Manakul et al., \"SelfCheckGPT: Zero-Resource Black-Box Hallucination Detection for Generative LLMs,\" EMNLP 2023. Uses sample consistency for hallucination detection on free-form outputs.\n\n**Uncertainty quantification (fundamental to the confidence estimation framing):**\n- Kuhn et al., \"Semantic Uncertainty: Linguistic Invariances for Uncertainty Estimation in Natural Language Generation,\" ICML 2023. Proposes semantic clustering to handle the exact problem of agreement measurement for complex outputs.\n- Jiang et al., \"How Can We Know When Language Models Know? On the Calibration of Language Models for Question Answering,\" TACL 2021. Verbalized confidence and its limitations.\n- Lin et al., \"Teaching Models to Express Their Uncertainty in Words,\" TMLR 2022.\n- Xiong et al., \"Can LLMs Express Their Uncertainty? An Empirical Evaluation of Confidence Elicitation in LLMs,\" ICLR 2024.\n\n**Calibration and selective prediction:**\n- Kadavath et al., \"Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know,\" 2022 — mentioned in the paper but only parenthetically.\n- Desai & Durrett, \"Calibration of Pre-trained Transformers,\" EMNLP 2020.\n- Kuleshov & Liang, \"Calibrated Structured Prediction,\" NeurIPS 2015.\n- Geifman & El-Yaniv, \"Selective Classification for Deep Neural Networks,\" NeurIPS 2017.\n- Fisch et al., \"Few-Shot Conformal Prediction with Auxiliary Tasks,\" ICML 2021.\n\n**Test-time scaling (the stated motivation):**\n- Snell et al., \"Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally Can Be More Effective Than Scaling Model Parameters,\" 2024.\n- Cobbe et al., \"Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems,\" 2021.\n- Lightman et al., \"Let's Verify Step by Step,\" ICLR 2024.\n\n**Structured prediction confidence:**\n- Culotta & McCallum, \"Confidence Estimation for Information Extraction,\" HLT-NAACL 2004.\n- Shelmanov et al., \"How Certain Is Your Transformer?\" EACL 2021.\n- Luo et al., \"Token-Level Uncertainty Estimation for Entity Extraction,\" AAAI 2023.\n\n**Negative results in NLP:**\n- Beyond Rogers 2020, there is a body of work on negative results in NLP replication studies that should be cited to position this paper properly within the tradition.\n\n### 2.3 Assessment\n\nThe related work is not just thin — it fails to engage with the most relevant prior work (USC), the theoretical frameworks that explain the observed phenomena (calibration, selective prediction, epistemic/aleatoric uncertainty decomposition), and the alternative methods that should serve as baselines (log-probability, semantic uncertainty, process reward models). This is the single most fixable weakness but also the most damaging to the paper's credibility as a venue-level contribution, because it suggests the authors are not aware of closely related work that would substantially change the framing.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Significance and Impact\n\n### 3.1 Target Audience\n\nThe paper targets practitioners considering consistency-based TTS for structured prediction. This audience is real but narrow — most IE practitioners use greedy decoding or beam search, and the TTS paradigm is primarily established for reasoning and code generation tasks. The paper's relevance depends on how many practitioners would have seriously considered applying self-consistency to IE; given the lack of prior work doing so, the audience may be smaller than the paper implies.\n\n### 3.2 Actionable Guidance\n\nThe practitioner guidelines (Section 8.1) require four conditions to all hold: mid-range base performance (F1 in [0.50, 0.80]), simple output structure (NER-like), stable generation format, and in-distribution data. These conditions are so restrictive that the practical guidance is effectively \"don't use consistency-based confidence in most realistic scenarios.\" This is a valid conclusion but provides limited constructive value. The paper does not propose alternative confidence signals for the settings where consistency fails, leaving practitioners with a prohibition but no path forward.\n\n### 3.3 Scale of Finding\n\nThe best-case positive result — SciERC NER with Qwen3-8B at N=8 yielding rho=0.38 — is itself a moderate correlation. The best-of-N selection fails to beat random selection (p=0.47). The paper thus demonstrates that even in the most favorable setting, the signal is too weak for practical use. This is an important finding, but one that could be communicated in 2-3 pages rather than a full paper, because the underlying mechanism (range restriction, base-rate effects) is well-understood.\n\n**Overall significance:** Low-to-moderate. The finding is valid and could save practitioners time, but the audience is narrow, the constructive guidance is minimal, and the explanatory mechanisms are not novel.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Framing and Narrative\n\n### 4.1 Negative-Result Framing\n\nThe negative-result framing is honest and, on the whole, well-executed. The paper does not try to hide failures or oversell marginal positives. The progression from \"SJ achieves moderate rho on one setting\" to \"this doesn't transfer\" to \"consistency can't beat random selection\" is clearly presented.\n\nHowever, the framing has a fundamental tension: the paper positions consistency-based confidence as a natural and reasonable approach for structured prediction (Section 1, paragraph 2), but the prior work section does not cite any work that has actually attempted this. If no one has tried consistency-based confidence for IE, the paper is disproving a hypothesis that was never held rather than overturning an established belief. This weakens the impact of the negative result — compare this to negative results that overturn widely-held assumptions (e.g., showing that a popular technique does not work as believed).\n\n### 4.2 Motivation Gap\n\nThe paper argues that self-consistency's success in reasoning tasks motivates extension to structured prediction, but this motivation is asserted rather than supported. The key differences between reasoning and structured prediction outputs — which the paper identifies in Section 8.2 (discrete vs. combinatorial output space, deterministic vs. partial convergence, random vs. systematic errors) — are so fundamental that one might argue the extension was never well-motivated in the first place. The paper would be strengthened by citing at least one work that has tried or advocated for consistency-based confidence in structured prediction, to establish that the hypothesis being tested is non-trivial.\n\n### 4.3 Comparison to Reasoning Tasks\n\nSection 8.2 identifies three properties that make structured prediction different: (a) combinatorial output space, (b) partial correctness, (c) systematic errors. This analysis is correct but brief (one paragraph) and does not connect to existing theoretical frameworks. The structured prediction calibration literature (Kuleshov & Liang 2015), conformal prediction for NLP (Fisch et al. 2021), and the epistemic/aleatoric uncertainty decomposition all provide formal frameworks for exactly these properties. The paper would benefit significantly from connecting its empirical observations to this theory.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Positioning Gaps\n\n### 5.1 Calibration and Selective Prediction\n\nThe paper is fundamentally about confidence estimation for structured outputs, yet it does not engage with the calibration literature at all. Calibration (is a confidence of 0.8 associated with 80% accuracy?) is the standard framework for evaluating confidence signals, and the paper uses only rank correlation (Spearman rho). There is no discussion of whether SJ or FK is well-calibrated, whether temperature scaling or Platt scaling could improve the signal, or how the consistency score could be incorporated into a selective prediction framework (abstain when confidence is low). This represents a major positioning gap.\n\n### 5.2 Alternative Confidence Methods\n\nThe paper compares only two metrics (SJ and FK) without discussing the rich landscape of alternative confidence methods:\n- **Log-probability**: cheapest, requires no additional inference\n- **Ensemble disagreement**: same cost as multi-sample but with diverse models\n- **Learned verifiers / reward models**: the dominant TTS paradigm\n- **Conformal prediction**: provides statistical guarantees on set coverage\n- **Semantic uncertainty / clustering**: handles complex output spaces\n\nThe paper's scope is narrow not because of the research question but because it only examines one dimension (metric design) of a multi-dimensional problem. A broader discussion of where consistency-based confidence fits in the landscape of confidence methods would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n### 5.3 SJ vs FK Narrowness\n\nThe paper frames the question as \"SJ vs FK\" when the broader question is whether any consistency-based approach works for structured prediction. FK is a weak baseline chosen for contrast, not because it represents the best surface-level agreement metric. Other set-agreement metrics (e.g., exact-match voting rate, entity-level majority voting, graph edit distance for RE) are not considered. The paper's negative conclusion (\"consistency fails\") is stronger than what the evidence supports — the evidence shows that two specific metrics (SJ and FK) fail, not that consistency-based approaches as a class are doomed.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n### Top 3 Strengths\n\n**S1. Thorough experimental coverage with honest reporting.** The paper tests across 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, 2 model families, 2 temperatures, N in {2,4,8,16}, and includes OOD evaluation, capability stratification, per-relation-type analysis, and selection curves. The comprehensive negative result, reported without spin, is scientifically valuable. The cross-seed stability analysis (Table cross-seed, std=0.009) adds further rigor.\n\n**S2. The best-of-N selection failure is a compelling demonstration.** Showing that SJ-based selection cannot beat random selection (p=0.47) despite substantial oracle headroom (7.5pp) is the single most impactful result. It translates the abstract \"rho is low\" into a concrete practical failure, and the juxtaposition with oracle performance makes the gap vivid.\n\n**S3. Error decomposition across tasks and datasets.** The three-dataset inverted-U pattern (cross-dataset) combined with the within-dataset capability stratification, per-relation-type breakdown showing all types fail uniformly, and compound error propagation analysis together provide a multi-faceted diagnostic that goes beyond simply reporting \"it doesn't work.\"\n\n### Top 3 Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Critical) Critically thin related work and missing baselines.** 10 references is inadequate by any standard for a top venue. Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., 2024), semantic uncertainty (Kuhn et al., 2023), and the calibration/selective prediction literature are entirely absent. Without comparison to log-probability, USC, or exact-match voting, the paper cannot determine whether SJ fails because consistency-based confidence is fundamentally unsuitable (the paper's claim) or because SJ is specifically a poor metric. This is the most damaging weakness because it undermines the generality of the paper's negative conclusion.\n\n**W2. (Critical) Low novelty — predictable failure with known explanations.** The failure modes (range restriction at ceiling, base-rate effects at floor, model-specific generation artifacts) are well-understood statistical phenomena. The paper rediscovers them in a specific context but does not connect to existing theory (calibration, epistemic vs. aleatoric uncertainty, restriction of range in correlation analysis). The inverted-U pattern is suggestive but not statistically significant within datasets (p=0.12 and p=0.38). The technical contribution (applying soft Jaccard as a self-agreement measure) is a straightforward metric substitution without algorithmic innovation or theoretical analysis.\n\n**W3. (Major) Overgeneralized negative conclusion.** The paper's title claims \"Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction,\" but the evidence shows that two specific metrics (SJ and FK) fail on 3+1 datasets with 2 models at 8B scale. The paper does not test stronger consistency variants (USC, semantic clustering, entity-level voting), larger models, or alternative confidence frameworks (log-prob, verifiers). Claiming that \"self-consistency fails\" when only one form of consistency was tested (and against only one weak baseline) overreaches. A more accurate title would be \"Soft Jaccard and Fleiss' Kappa Are Weak Confidence Signals for Information Extraction.\"\n\n---\n\n## 7. Score\n\n**Preliminary Score: 2/6 (Reject)**\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper addresses a real question and provides thorough experiments, but it falls below the acceptance threshold on multiple critical dimensions:\n\n1. **Novelty is insufficient** (1.5/6). The technical contribution is metric substitution. The failure modes are predictable from known statistical principles. The inverted-U pattern is suggestive but not statistically established. The paper does not introduce new concepts, algorithms, or theoretical frameworks.\n\n2. **Related work is critically inadequate** (1/6). 10 references for a venue-level submission is disqualifying. The most relevant prior work (USC) is not cited. The calibration, selective prediction, and uncertainty quantification literatures are not engaged with at all.\n\n3. **The negative conclusion is overgeneralized** relative to the evidence. Testing two metrics against each other (without any external baselines like log-probability) and concluding that \"self-consistency fails for structured prediction\" is a logical overreach. The paper cannot distinguish \"consistency fails\" from \"SJ/FK are poor metrics.\"\n\n4. **Significance is limited.** The finding saves practitioners from one specific dead end but provides no constructive alternative. The audience is narrow because consistency-based TTS for IE was not an established practice in the first place.\n\n5. **The experimental execution is the paper's one clear strength**, but strong experiments in service of a thin contribution with poor positioning do not clear the 25% acceptance bar.\n\nThe gap between this paper and acceptability is substantial: it would require (a) tripling the reference list with proper engagement, (b) adding external baselines (log-probability at minimum) to scope whether the failure is specific to SJ or general to consistency, (c) connecting the failure modes to existing theoretical frameworks, and (d) either providing constructive alternatives or narrowing the title/claims to match the evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** How does SJ compare against sequence log-probability as a selection signal? Log-probability requires zero additional inference and is the natural baseline for any confidence estimation approach. If log-prob also fails, the negative conclusion is much stronger. If log-prob succeeds, the failure is specific to consistency-based approaches.\n\n**Q2.** Are you aware of Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024)? USC extends self-consistency to non-votable structured outputs using LLM-as-judge for pairwise equivalence assessment. This appears to be a strictly more general approach that addresses the core limitation you identify (the difficulty of measuring agreement between complex outputs). How does your work position against USC?\n\n**Q3.** The ceiling effect on CoNLL (F1=0.81, rho=0.22) and floor effect on WNUT (F1=0.42, rho=0.19) are standard consequences of range restriction in correlation analysis. Can you articulate what your paper contributes beyond demonstrating this well-known statistical phenomenon in a new setting?\n\n**Q4.** The paper's title claims \"Self-Consistency Fails\" but only tests two specific metrics (SJ and FK). Have you considered entity-level majority voting (selecting the most commonly predicted entity set), semantic clustering of outputs (a la Kuhn et al. 2023), or graph edit distance for RE? How do you justify the generalization from \"SJ and FK fail\" to \"self-consistency fails\"?\n\n**Q5.** The capability stratification analysis (Table 6) shows the inverted-U pattern is not statistically significant (NER p=0.12, RE p=0.38). Given that this is positioned as a core finding, can you provide additional evidence — e.g., from a larger dataset, or from stratifying on an independent difficulty proxy rather than the variable being correlated (greedy F1)?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23158,"cache_read_input_tokens":78449,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":23158,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"de95fe7a-93b9-41a2-9b31-2d100a0ef440"}
