{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2271c80e6d8c2759","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKfzCDwhxMENhz8xdBgfGN","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":96968,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":235690},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"8a8dd0c6-0949-4fc0-a407-6769d8b793f6","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NiDGY44rykdCq4oGyDFhJS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EzskT2XXQXCEiT1r8wfZZx","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260514T093336","overall_score":"weak reject (3/6)","report":"# Review Report — Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Paper:** \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction\"\n**Tier:** Worst\n**Dream ID:** dream_20260514T093336\n**Review Focus:** Methodological soundness, experimental design, statistical rigor, reproducibility\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n### Metric Definitions\n\nThe two core metrics — Structural Soft Jaccard (SJ) and Fleiss' Kappa (FK) — are conceptually well-motivated but their formal presentation has problems.\n\n**SJ formula vs. implementation mismatch (critical).** The paper defines SJ in Section 3 via a simplified set-overlap formula (Eq. 1: `|y_i ∩ y_j| / |y_i ∪ y_j|`), but the actual implementation (`consistency.py`) uses a substantially different algorithm: (a) entities are grouped by type, (b) within each type group, Hungarian (one-to-one) matching via `scipy.optimize.linear_sum_assignment` is applied on character-level span Jaccard, (c) the denominator is `max(|g_a|, |g_b|)` per type group, not set union. These are different algorithms with different mathematical properties. The paper formula permits many-to-one matching; the implementation enforces one-to-one. The denominators differ. Anyone reproducing from the paper equations would obtain different results. This is a serious reproducibility concern that must be fixed.\n\n**FK as a surface baseline is poorly motivated for this task.** FK operates on presence/absence of `(text, type)` surface keys (per the implementation), not on BIO token sequences as the paper implies. This distinction matters: the FK implementation is actually closer to an exact-match set agreement metric than a token-level one. The paper should describe the actual FK formulation used.\n\n**Empty-set handling introduces bias.** Both SJ and FK return 1.0 when both samples produce empty outputs. For RE where 42-44% of instances have all-zero F1, this means a large fraction of instances receive perfect consistency scores (SJ=1.0, FK=1.0) paired with F1=0. This creates a strong cluster at (consistency=1, F1=0) that systematically drags down the Spearman correlation. The paper acknowledges this through \"conditional\" analysis but does not formally analyze how the empty-set convention affects the correlation structure.\n\n### Is Spearman rho the Right Evaluation Measure?\n\nSpearman rho is a reasonable starting point but insufficient as the sole evaluation metric. Several alternatives would strengthen the analysis:\n\n- **AUROC**: Can the metric distinguish \"good\" from \"bad\" outputs at a threshold? This is the operationally relevant question for deployment.\n- **Calibration**: Is SJ=0.8 associated with ~80% accuracy? A well-calibrated confidence signal is more useful than one with high rank correlation but poor calibration.\n- **Kendall's tau**: More robust to tied ranks, which are common when entity sets are small.\n- **Precision@k / NDCG**: For the selection use-case, top-k ranking quality matters more than global rank correlation.\n\nRelying solely on rho leaves open the possibility that SJ has moderate rank correlation but poor practical discrimination.\n\n### Methodological Gaps\n\n1. **No model-internal baselines.** The paper omits the cheapest and most natural confidence signal: sequence log-probability (or mean token-level log-prob). This requires zero additional inference passes. If SJ cannot beat log-prob, then the 8-16x inference cost of multi-sample consistency is unjustified, and the negative result is trivially explained by \"consistency is an expensive way to get a weak signal.\"\n\n2. **No exact-match voting baseline.** The direct analogue of Wang et al.'s self-consistency for structured outputs — the fraction of N samples producing identical output sets — is absent. This is the most natural first baseline.\n\n3. **No Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024) comparison.** USC extends self-consistency to non-votable structured outputs using LLM-as-judge for pairwise equivalence assessment. This is the single most relevant prior work and is entirely absent.\n\n4. **Evaluation uses greedy F1 as the correlation target.** The consistency score is computed from T>0 samples but correlated with greedy (T=0) F1. This is a subtle design choice that is never justified. Why not correlate with: (a) mean sample F1, (b) max sample F1, or (c) leave-one-out sample F1? The choice of greedy F1 means we are asking \"does inter-sample agreement predict the quality of a *different* decoding mode,\" which is a harder and potentially less relevant question than \"does agreement predict quality of the samples themselves.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Design\n\n### Coverage\n\nThe experimental matrix covers 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, 2 models, 2 temperatures, 2 N values, capability stratification, OOD evaluation, per-relation-type analysis, cross-seed stability, and best-of-N selection. For a negative-result paper, this breadth is commendable and substantially exceeds typical empirical studies.\n\n### Critical Design Issues\n\n**Table 1 uses inconsistent temperatures across datasets (major confound).** SciERC uses T=1.0 while CoNLL and WNUT use T=0.7. Table 3 shows that temperature affects rho (NER rho_SJ: 0.376 at T=0.7 vs. 0.383 at T=1.0). This confounds the cross-dataset comparison. The paper's central negative claim — \"SJ advantage does not transfer\" — rests on comparing numbers obtained under different experimental conditions. A fair comparison requires all datasets at the same temperature, or at minimum, reporting both temperatures for all datasets.\n\nThe effect may be small for NER (delta = -0.007), but the principle of controlled comparison is violated. For a negative-result paper that claims generalization failure, this confound is especially damaging: reviewers must be confident that observed differences reflect genuine dataset effects, not temperature artifacts.\n\n**Only one RE dataset limits diagnostic claims.** All RE analysis relies on SciERC (397 instances, 7 relation types). The \"compound error propagation\" explanation is plausible and internally consistent, but it is an interpretation of one dataset. A second RE dataset (TACRED, DocRED, or ACE05) would substantially strengthen the diagnostic claims. As written, the RE findings are suggestive but dataset-specific.\n\n**WNUT-17 is a questionable choice for \"floor effect\" demonstration.** WNUT-17 (emerging/novel entities from social media) is designed to be adversarial — models are expected to struggle with unseen entity types. Using it to demonstrate that consistency fails on hard tasks risks a tautology: of course consistency fails when the model fundamentally cannot do the task. A more informative comparison would use a dataset where the model achieves mid-range performance (F1 ~ 0.50-0.60), which is the alleged \"sweet spot.\" The three datasets conveniently sit at F1 = 0.42, 0.67, and 0.81, but the extreme values are at the boundaries where any confidence signal would be expected to fail.\n\n**OOD evaluation is weak.** The OOD test uses the WNUT-trained model (already weak: F1=0.418 in-domain) on out-of-distribution data, yielding F1=0.295. This compounds floor effects — the model is already barely functional in-domain. A more meaningful OOD evaluation would use a strong in-domain model (e.g., CoNLL-trained) on a different news corpus to test whether the consistency signal degrades under mild distribution shift.\n\n### Baselines for Best-of-N Selection\n\nThe best-of-N selection experiment (Table 7) compares SJ, FK, random, and oracle. Missing baselines:\n\n- **Greedy-plus-random**: Simply use greedy when available, random when not. This is the deploy-time default.\n- **Log-probability selection**: Select the sample with the highest sequence log-probability. Zero additional compute beyond generation.\n- **Length-based heuristics**: Longer/shorter outputs may systematically differ in quality.\n\nWithout these, we cannot assess whether the failure of SJ-based selection is specific to SJ or reflects a general limitation of post-hoc selection signals for structured prediction.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Statistical Rigor\n\n### Strengths\n\n- Cross-seed stability analysis (Table 6) with 3 seeds showing std=0.009 is a good practice, though limited in scope (only N=16 NER on SciERC).\n- Bootstrap 95% CI for per-relation-type SJ-FK difference ([-0.08, +0.05]) is appropriately reported.\n- The p=0.47 for SJ vs. random selection is correctly used to demonstrate non-significance.\n\n### Concerns\n\n**Only 3 seeds is insufficient for stability claims.** Three seeds provide a point estimate and standard deviation but do not allow meaningful confidence interval construction. Five seeds would be the minimum for a publishable stability analysis; 10 would be ideal for a paper whose conclusions depend on the magnitude of rho.\n\n**Jonckheere-Terpstra tests for inverted-U are underpowered (NER p=0.12, RE p=0.38).** The paper claims an inverted-U pattern but the formal trend test fails to reach significance for either task. The paper acknowledges this (\"suggestive but cannot be precisely delineated\") but then uses the inverted-U as a central organizing principle for the discussion and practitioner guidelines. This overstates the evidence. The qualitative observation of an inverted-U across three datasets (Section 4.2) is even weaker — 3 data points confounded by domain, entity types, annotation quality, and model performance cannot establish a functional relationship.\n\n**No multiple comparison correction.** The paper reports numerous correlation tests (2 metrics x 3 datasets x 2 conditions x 2 temperatures x 2 N values + per-relation-type + stratification). No correction for multiple comparisons (Bonferroni, Holm, or FDR) is applied. While the primary SciERC NER finding (p < 1e-17) would survive any correction, the per-stratum and per-relation-type analyses would not.\n\n**Capability stratification introduces circularity.** Stratifying by greedy F1 and then computing rho within strata is methodologically problematic: the stratification variable (greedy F1) is the same variable being correlated against. This restricts the range of F1 within each stratum, mechanically compressing rho. The inverted-U could be partly an artifact of range restriction at the extremes. The paper should verify the pattern using an independent difficulty proxy (sentence length, entity count, entity type distribution).\n\n**N-scaling \"degradation\" may be artifactual.** The claim that rho_SJ decreases from N=8 (0.383) to N=16 (0.362) is presented as evidence that \"additional samples introduce noise.\" But the difference is only 0.021 with cross-seed std=0.009, meaning the decline is about 2 standard deviations — borderline significant but hardly definitive. An alternative explanation is that N=8 and N=16 use different random seeds for sampling, and the difference reflects seed variance rather than a systematic N effect. A stronger test would use the first 8 of 16 samples for N=8 evaluation, eliminating the seed confound.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Reproducibility\n\n### What Is Provided\n\n- Core metrics code (consistency.py, evaluation.py) is available and well-documented.\n- The run_mvp_pilot.py script provides a complete pipeline from data loading through evaluation.\n- Hyperparameters for sampling (N, T, max_tokens) are specified.\n\n### What Is Missing\n\n1. **LoRA fine-tuning details.** The paper says models are \"fine-tuned with LoRA\" but does not report: LoRA rank (r), alpha, target modules, learning rate, number of epochs, batch size, training data size, or evaluation loss. These are essential for reproducing the base models.\n\n2. **Constrained decoding details.** XGrammar is mentioned but the grammar specification, handling of thinking tokens (Qwen3's `<think>` block), and exact generation parameters are not provided.\n\n3. **Data preprocessing.** The conversion from standard dataset formats (BIO for CoNLL, JSON for SciERC) to the unified IE format is not described. Character offset computation is non-trivial (the memory.md notes a span realignment issue with 16.7% accuracy on raw offsets).\n\n4. **Test set sizes vary but are not all reported.** SciERC: 529 instances (mentioned in tables). CoNLL: not specified in the worst-case paper (3,453 in best-case). WNUT: not specified.\n\n5. **Formula-implementation discrepancy** (detailed in Section 1 above) means the paper alone is insufficient for reproduction.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Specific Concerns\n\n### Table 1 Temperature Inconsistency\n\nThis is the paper's most damaging methodological issue. The core cross-dataset comparison uses T=1.0 for SciERC but T=0.7 for CoNLL and WNUT. The paper acknowledges this in a table footnote but does not discuss its implications. Table 3 shows temperature effects are small for NER (delta = -0.007) but this was measured only on SciERC. The temperature effect on CoNLL and WNUT is unknown — it could be larger on datasets where the model is at the extremes of the capability range.\n\nFor a negative-result paper arguing that \"the SJ advantage does not transfer across datasets,\" maintaining identical experimental conditions across datasets is not optional — it is the minimum requirement for the claim to be credible. I would require either: (a) all datasets at the same T, or (b) both T values reported for all datasets with a formal interaction test.\n\n### Only 3 Seeds for N=16 Stability\n\nThree seeds provide a rough estimate of variance but do not support the paper's quantitative claims. The key concern: the N=16 results show rho_SJ = {0.371, 0.354, 0.362}, with the highest value (0.371) being 0.012 below the N=8 value (0.383). With only 3 seeds, we cannot determine whether the apparent N=8→16 degradation is real or within normal fluctuation. Five to ten seeds would resolve this ambiguity.\n\n### Only 8B Models — Generalizability\n\nThe limitation to 8B models is acknowledged but its implications are understated. Two specific concerns:\n\n1. **Larger models may have qualitatively different sampling behavior.** 70B+ models produce more stable outputs with less format variation, potentially eliminating the \"boundary wobble\" confound identified for LLaMA-3.1-8B. The negative results on LLaMA (SJ < FK) may be specific to small models with poor format adherence.\n\n2. **The \"consistently wrong\" failure mode may be less prevalent in stronger models.** The floor effect (WNUT F1=0.42) partly reflects model weakness. A stronger model achieving F1=0.60 on WNUT would shift the dataset into the alleged \"sweet spot,\" potentially invalidating the negative conclusion.\n\nThe paper's practitioner guidelines (\"if F1 < 0.50 or F1 > 0.80, consistency is uninformative\") are stated as general principles but are calibrated to 8B models. They may not hold for 70B+ models where the F1 distribution shifts upward across all datasets.\n\n### Inverted-U Claim with Non-Significant Trend Tests\n\nThe inverted-U pattern is the paper's central diagnostic finding, used to motivate the three failure modes (ceiling, floor, confounds) and the practitioner guidelines. However:\n\n- Within-dataset stratification: JT test p=0.12 (NER), p=0.38 (RE) — both non-significant.\n- Across-dataset: 3 data points (F1=0.42, 0.67, 0.81) with confounded conditions.\n- The pattern is consistent with range restriction artifacts (see Section 3).\n\nThe paper should either: (a) present the inverted-U as a descriptive observation rather than a confirmed finding, or (b) provide a more rigorous test (e.g., quadratic regression with proper controls, additional datasets in the mid-range).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n### Top 3 Strengths\n\n**S1. Comprehensive experimental matrix with honest negative-result framing.** The paper covers 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, 2 models, 2 temperatures, 2 sample budgets, capability stratification, per-type analysis, OOD, and selection curves. The honest acknowledgment that results are \"predominantly negative\" and the attempt to explain *why* rather than just reporting failure is scientifically mature.\n\n**S2. The three failure modes framework is genuinely useful.** Ceiling effects (high-accuracy regimes), floor effects (knowledge gaps), and model-specific confounds (format instability, boundary wobble) are clearly articulated explanatory mechanisms. Even if the quantitative support is imperfect, these categories provide practical diagnostic value for practitioners considering consistency-based confidence.\n\n**S3. Best-of-N selection experiment provides the definitive practical test.** Rather than stopping at correlation analysis, the paper demonstrates that the moderate correlation does not translate to practical selection gains (SJ vs. random: p=0.47). This is the paper's strongest result — it directly answers the question \"should practitioners use this?\" with a clear negative.\n\n### Top 3 Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Missing critical baselines make the negative result uninterpretable (critical).** Without comparison to log-probability, exact-match voting, or USC, we cannot distinguish between \"consistency is a bad confidence signal for structured prediction\" and \"SJ and FK are both bad implementations of consistency as a confidence signal.\" The paper's core claim — that self-consistency *fails* for structured prediction — requires ruling out that better consistency operationalizations would succeed. Log-probability, in particular, is the elephant in the room: if it matches or beats SJ at 1/16th the cost, the negative result is about cost-effectiveness, not about consistency per se.\n\n**W2. Inconsistent experimental conditions undermine the cross-dataset comparison (major).** The temperature confound in Table 1 (T=1.0 for SciERC, T=0.7 for CoNLL/WNUT) is the most concrete methodological flaw. Combined with mixed N across some analyses, the paper fails to maintain the controlled conditions necessary for its generalization-failure argument. A negative-result paper faces higher scrutiny on experimental rigor precisely because the absence of effect must be convincingly demonstrated.\n\n**W3. Formula-implementation discrepancy undermines reproducibility (major).** The paper presents one algorithm (symmetric max-match with union denominator); the code implements another (type-grouped Hungarian matching with max denominator per group). This is not a minor notational issue — the algorithms have different matching behavior and normalization. Any reproduction attempt from the paper alone would yield different results.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Score: 3 / 6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper addresses a relevant and timely question — whether self-consistency extends from reasoning to structured prediction — and provides an honest negative answer backed by extensive experiments. The three failure modes framework and the best-of-N futility demonstration are genuine contributions. The experimental breadth is impressive for a negative-result study.\n\nHowever, three issues prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) **The negative result is uninterpretable without stronger baselines.** Comparing only SJ against FK (a weak baseline never designed for this task) tells us that SJ is moderately better than FK in some conditions and worse in others. It does not tell us whether *consistency itself* fails for structured prediction, or whether SJ and FK are simply poor operationalizations. Log-probability, exact-match voting, and USC are all necessary to properly scope the negative claim. Without them, the paper's title-level conclusion (\"self-consistency fails\") is unsupported — it may be that *these two metrics* fail while the principle remains valid.\n\n(2) **Methodological flaws in the cross-dataset comparison.** The temperature confound in Table 1 directly undermines the paper's most important argument (non-transferability across datasets). For a negative-result paper, controlled experimental conditions are not negotiable.\n\n(3) **The inverted-U pattern is presented as a finding but lacks statistical support.** Both formal trend tests fail (p=0.12, p=0.38), the cross-dataset version has 3 confounded points, and the within-dataset version suffers from range-restriction circularity. Yet this pattern drives the discussion, guidelines, and framing. The gap between evidence and claims is too large.\n\nThe paper is between Reject and Weak Accept. The experimental thoroughness and honest framing push toward Weak Accept; the missing baselines, temperature confound, and overstated inverted-U push toward Reject. On balance, Weak Reject — fixable issues but currently below the bar for a top venue.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** (a) Add log-probability and exact-match voting baselines for all datasets. If the negative result persists against these stronger baselines, the conclusion becomes far more compelling. (b) Standardize temperature to T=1.0 or report both temperatures for all datasets. (c) Downgrade the inverted-U from a \"finding\" to a \"descriptive observation\" or add statistical power. (d) Fix the formula-implementation discrepancy. (e) Expand the reference list to 25+ and engage with USC, semantic uncertainty, and LLM calibration literature.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1. How does SJ compare against sequence log-probability as a selection signal?** Log-prob requires zero additional inference cost. If SJ cannot beat log-prob, the practical implication is simple: \"don't bother with multi-sample consistency for structured prediction — just use log-prob.\" This would substantially sharpen the negative message.\n\n**Q2. Why are different temperatures used across datasets in Table 1?** Was this a deliberate design choice (if so, what is the justification?) or an artifact of sequential experimentation? Can you provide SciERC results at T=0.7 and CoNLL/WNUT results at T=1.0 for a fair comparison?\n\n**Q3. The N=8 to N=16 degradation (rho 0.383 → 0.362) — did you verify this using the first 8 of the 16 samples?** Using independent 8-sample sets confounds the N effect with seed variance. A nested comparison (subsample the 16 to 8) would cleanly test whether additional samples hurt.\n\n**Q4. The inverted-U analysis stratifies on greedy F1 and then correlates consistency with greedy F1 within strata. Have you verified the pattern holds when stratifying on an independent proxy (sentence length, entity count)?** The current approach introduces range restriction that mechanically compresses rho at the extremes.\n\n**Q5. For the empty-empty case (both samples produce zero entities), both SJ and FK return 1.0. What fraction of all pairwise comparisons fall into this case for RE, and how does removing these pairs (rather than these instances) affect the correlation?** The current \"conditional\" analysis removes instances where ALL samples are F1=0, but the more granular issue is that individual sample pairs with both-empty inflate the mean SJ even for instances where some samples are non-empty.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":717,"cache_read_input_tokens":95613,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":717,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKfzCDwhxMENhz8xdBgfGN","session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"8100f1cf-4dc5-4ea4-bedd-cc2b8d75c27a"}
