{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0167Y42ihqJcnBJuj2T61bym","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 2/6 (Reject)** for the worst-tier paper \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction.\"\n\n**Core issues identified across the R2 review dimensions:**\n\n1. **Novelty (1.5/6):** The failure modes (ceiling, floor, model confounds) are predictable from well-known statistical phenomena (range restriction, base-rate effects). The inverted-U pattern is not statistically significant (p=0.12, p=0.38). The technical contribution is straightforward metric substitution (soft Jaccard applied as a self-agreement measure) with no algorithmic innovation or theoretical grounding.\n\n2. **Related Work (1/6):** Only 10 references -- critically insufficient for a venue-level submission. Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024) is the most directly relevant prior work and is entirely absent. The calibration, selective prediction, semantic uncertainty, and conformal prediction literatures are not engaged with at all.\n\n3. **Overgeneralized Claims:** The title claims \"self-consistency fails\" but only two specific metrics (SJ and FK) are tested against each other. Without external baselines (log-probability, USC, exact-match voting), the paper cannot distinguish \"consistency fundamentally fails for structured prediction\" from \"SJ and FK are poor metrics.\"\n\n4. **Limited Significance:** The practitioner guidelines amount to \"don't use consistency-based confidence in most settings.\" No constructive alternatives are proposed, and the audience is narrow since consistency-based TTS for IE was not established practice.\n\n**Key strengths:** Thorough experimental coverage (3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, 2 models, temperature/N-scaling), honest reporting, and the best-of-N selection failure (SJ cannot beat random, p=0.47) is a compelling concrete demonstration.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2059b4ae02bc538c (use SendMessage with to: 'a2059b4ae02bc538c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 107499\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 208605</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"8dd3d33d-ed31-41c7-9113-6ac951a253a4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:26:16.994Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work/positioning**. Review the paper as if for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction\"\n\nThe paper systematically evaluates two consistency metrics—structural soft Jaccard (SJ) and surface-level Fleiss' Kappa (FK)—as confidence estimators for information extraction. It tests across 3 NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), 1 RE dataset (SciERC), 2 model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and sampling budgets N∈{2,4,8,16}. The findings are predominantly negative: SJ achieves moderate ρ=0.38 only on one dataset-model combination (SciERC NER + Qwen3-8B) and this doesn't transfer. Best-of-N selection fails to beat random selection (p=0.47). Three failure modes identified: ceiling effect, floor effect, model-specific confounds. The paper identifies an inverted-U pattern between model capability and consistency signal strength.\n\n## Paper Structure and Content\n\n### Introduction (Section 1)\n- Motivates the extension of self-consistency from reasoning tasks to structured prediction\n- Claims to be the \"first systematic negative result on consistency-based confidence for structured prediction\"\n- Three contributions: (1) systematic negative result, (2) error decomposition framework, (3) practitioner guidelines\n\n### Related Work (Section 2) — very brief, ~1/2 page\nFour short paragraphs:\n- Self-consistency for reasoning: cites Wang et al. 2023, Brown et al. 2024, Chen et al. 2023\n- Confidence estimation for LLMs: cites Kadavath et al. 2022, mentions verbalized confidence, token entropy, ensemble disagreement\n- Structured prediction with LLMs: cites Li et al. 2023, Wadhwa et al. 2023\n- Negative results in NLP: cites Rogers 2020\n\nTotal references: 10\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Defines SJ (soft Jaccard) and FK (Fleiss' Kappa)\n- Uses Spearman ρ between consistency and F1 as evaluation\n- Tests on 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, 2 models, N∈{2,4,8,16}, T∈{0.7,1.0}\n\n### Results (Sections 4-7)\n- Section 4: Cross-dataset fragility (SJ advantage confined to SciERC), inverted-U pattern, OOD collapse\n- Section 5: Temperature is not a rescue\n- Section 6: Capability stratification (inverted-U confirmed qualitatively but not statistically significant)\n- Section 7: Analysis—sampling saturation, selection curves, error propagation in RE, model-specific confounds\n\n### Discussion (Section 8)\n- Practitioner guidelines: 4 conditions for consistency to work\n- Why structured prediction differs from reasoning (3 properties violated)\n- Limitations acknowledged: only 8B models, specific SJ formulation, English only, statistical power issues\n\n### Conclusion (Section 9)\n- Summary of negative findings and call for more robust confidence signals\n\n## Experiment Context\nThe paper was designed as a negative-result analytical paper: \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction.\" The narrative was reframed from a positive claim about SJ superiority to a systematic investigation of failure modes. Key experiments include N-scaling, cross-dataset transfer, temperature sensitivity, capability stratification, multi-model comparison, best-of-N selection curves, per-relation-type analysis, and OOD robustness.\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: \n   - Is the research question novel? Has anyone studied consistency-based confidence for structured prediction before?\n   - Are the failure modes (ceiling, floor, model confounds) novel insights or predictable outcomes?\n   - Is the inverted-U finding a genuine contribution?\n   - How does this compare to general findings about self-consistency limitations?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**:\n   - Only 10 references total. Is this adequate for a venue-level submission?\n   - Missing areas: uncertainty quantification for structured prediction, calibration literature, other test-time scaling methods, selective prediction, conformal prediction for NLP\n   - Are there works on self-consistency limitations that should be cited?\n   - Missing: Jiang et al. on verbalized uncertainty, Lin et al. on teaching models to express uncertainty, Kuhn et al. on semantic uncertainty\n\n3. **Significance and Impact**:\n   - Who benefits from this paper? How large is the potential audience?\n   - Does the paper provide sufficient actionable guidance beyond \"don't use consistency\"?\n   - Is the \"practitioner guidelines\" contribution strong enough given how restrictive the conditions are?\n\n4. **Framing and Narrative**:\n   - Is the negative-result framing convincing?\n   - Does the paper adequately motivate why anyone would expect consistency to work for structured prediction in the first place?\n   - Is the comparison to reasoning tasks (Section 8.2) compelling?\n\n5. **Positioning Gaps**:\n   - Is there a missed opportunity to connect to calibration/selective prediction literature?\n   - Should the paper discuss alternative confidence methods more thoroughly?\n   - Is the paper too narrowly focused on SJ vs FK when the broader question is about any consistency-based approach?\n\n6. **Strengths and Weaknesses**: List top 3 strengths and top 3 weaknesses.\n\n7. **Score**: Provide your preliminary score (1-6) with justification.\n\n8. **Questions for Authors**: List 3-5 questions you would ask.\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers. Be rigorous—negative result papers need strong positioning and thorough literature coverage to justify venue-level acceptance.","agentId":"a2059b4ae02bc538c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 2/6 (Reject)** for the worst-tier paper \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction.\"\n\n**Core issues identified across the R2 review dimensions:**\n\n1. **Novelty (1.5/6):** The failure modes (ceiling, floor, model confounds) are predictable from well-known statistical phenomena (range restriction, base-rate effects). The inverted-U pattern is not statistically significant (p=0.12, p=0.38). The technical contribution is straightforward metric substitution (soft Jaccard applied as a self-agreement measure) with no algorithmic innovation or theoretical grounding.\n\n2. **Related Work (1/6):** Only 10 references -- critically insufficient for a venue-level submission. Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024) is the most directly relevant prior work and is entirely absent. The calibration, selective prediction, semantic uncertainty, and conformal prediction literatures are not engaged with at all.\n\n3. **Overgeneralized Claims:** The title claims \"self-consistency fails\" but only two specific metrics (SJ and FK) are tested against each other. Without external baselines (log-probability, USC, exact-match voting), the paper cannot distinguish \"consistency fundamentally fails for structured prediction\" from \"SJ and FK are poor metrics.\"\n\n4. **Limited Significance:** The practitioner guidelines amount to \"don't use consistency-based confidence in most settings.\" No constructive alternatives are proposed, and the audience is narrow since consistency-based TTS for IE was not established practice.\n\n**Key strengths:** Thorough experimental coverage (3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, 2 models, temperature/N-scaling), honest reporting, and the best-of-N selection failure (SJ cannot beat random, p=0.47) is a compelling concrete demonstration.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":208605,"totalTokens":107499,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5391,"cache_read_input_tokens":101607,"output_tokens":500,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5391},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":101607,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5391,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5391,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
