{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKfzCDwhxMENhz8xdBgfGN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe review covers the \"worst\" tier paper, \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction,\" which presents a predominantly negative result on using SJ and FK as confidence estimators for IE across 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, and 2 model families.\n\n**Top strengths identified:**\n1. Comprehensive experimental matrix with honest negative-result framing (11 experiments covering datasets, models, temperatures, N-scaling, stratification, OOD, selection curves)\n2. Three failure modes framework (ceiling, floor, model-specific confounds) provides genuinely useful diagnostic categories\n3. Best-of-N selection experiment (SJ vs. random p=0.47) delivers the definitive practical test\n\n**Top weaknesses identified:**\n1. **Missing critical baselines** — no comparison to log-probability, exact-match voting, or Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024). Without these, the paper cannot distinguish \"consistency fails for structured prediction\" from \"SJ and FK are poor operationalizations\"\n2. **Temperature confound in the cross-dataset table** — T=1.0 for SciERC but T=0.7 for CoNLL/WNUT undermines the non-transferability argument\n3. **Formula-implementation discrepancy** — paper describes symmetric max-match; code uses type-grouped Hungarian matching with different denominator\n4. **Inverted-U pattern lacks statistical support** — Jonckheere-Terpstra tests non-significant (p=0.12, p=0.38) yet the pattern drives the discussion and practitioner guidelines\n5. **Only 3 seeds** and **Spearman rho as sole evaluation metric** limit the statistical conclusions\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2271c80e6d8c2759 (use SendMessage with to: 'a2271c80e6d8c2759' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 102761\ntool_uses: 26\nduration_ms: 254125</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37f786ee-7832-4fe8-ada0-13058d333aba","uuid":"92468ae7-b62d-455f-86f9-0162e08e3407","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:26:34.733Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a blind peer review of an ML paper. Your focus areas are **methods** and **experiments**. Review the paper as if for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction\"\n\nThe paper systematically evaluates two consistency metrics—structural soft Jaccard (SJ) and surface-level Fleiss' Kappa (FK)—as confidence estimators for information extraction. It tests across 3 NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), 1 RE dataset (SciERC), 2 model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and sampling budgets N∈{2,4,8,16}. The findings are predominantly negative: SJ achieves moderate ρ=0.38 only on one dataset-model combination (SciERC NER + Qwen3-8B) and this doesn't transfer. Best-of-N selection fails to beat random selection (p=0.47). Three failure modes identified: ceiling effect, floor effect, model-specific confounds. Inverted-U pattern noted.\n\n## Key Paper Content\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Task: NER and RE. For each test instance, generate N stochastic samples from fine-tuned LLM at temperature T>0.\n- Metrics: SJ (soft Jaccard, set-level overlap) vs FK (Fleiss' Kappa, token-level agreement)\n- Evaluation: Spearman rank correlation ρ between consistency scores and instance-level F1\n- Datasets: SciERC (scientific, 6 entity types, 7 relation types), CoNLL-2003 (news, 4 entity types), WNUT-17 (social media, 6 entity types)\n- Models: Qwen3-8B (LoRA fine-tuned on each dataset), LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (LoRA on SciERC)\n- Default: N=8, T=1.0. Also test N=16, T=0.7.\n\n### Core Results\n**Table 1 (Cross-dataset NER):** SciERC: ρ_SJ=0.383, ρ_FK=0.259, Δ=+0.124 | CoNLL: ρ_SJ=0.220, ρ_FK=0.240, Δ=-0.020 | WNUT: ρ_SJ=0.190, ρ_FK=0.150, Δ=+0.040. Note: T=1.0 for SciERC but T=0.7 for CoNLL and WNUT.\n\n**Table 2 (OOD):** In-domain (WNUT): F1=0.418, ρ_SJ=0.190 | OOD: F1=0.295, ρ_SJ=0.120\n\n**Table 3 (Temperature):** NER ρ_SJ at T=0.7: 0.376 vs T=1.0: 0.383 (Δ=-0.007). RE ρ_SJ(cond) at T=0.7: 0.280 vs T=1.0: 0.316 (Δ=-0.036).\n\n**Table 4 (Capability stratification):** Inverted-U pattern in ρ_SJ by F1 stratum, but Jonckheere-Terpstra tests not significant (NER p=0.12, RE p=0.38).\n\n**Table 5 (N-scaling):** N=8→16: NER ρ_SJ drops 0.383→0.362, RE ρ_SJ drops 0.244→0.231.\n\n**Table 6 (Cross-seed):** N=16 NER: mean ρ_SJ=0.362±0.009 across 3 seeds.\n\n**Table 7 (Best-of-N selection):** At N=16: Oracle=0.742, SJ=0.679, FK=0.684, Random=0.681. SJ vs random p=0.47.\n\n**Table 8 (Per-relation-type):** All types ρ_SJ ∈ [0.09, 0.28]. Bootstrap 95% CI for SJ-FK: [-0.08, +0.05].\n\n**Table 9 (RE N-scaling):** N=8: ρ_SJ=0.244, N=16: ρ_SJ=0.231. Bootstrap CI [0.18, 0.28].\n\n**Table 10 (Model comparison):** Qwen3-8B: ρ_SJ=0.383, ρ_FK=0.259 | LLaMA-3.1-8B: ρ_SJ=0.280, ρ_FK=0.310. LLaMA has 12 parse failures vs 0 for Qwen.\n\n### Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 11 experiments:\n- exp-001: NER N=16 on SciERC (3 seeds) ✓ covered\n- exp-002: Fine-tune on CoNLL-2003 + NER pilot ✓ covered\n- exp-003: Fine-tune on WNUT-17 + NER pilot ✓ covered\n- exp-004: Per-relation-type consistency breakdown ✓ covered\n- exp-005: Temperature sensitivity T=0.7 vs T=1.0 ✓ covered\n- exp-006: Best-of-N selection (SJ vs FK vs random vs greedy) ✓ covered\n- exp-007: LLaMA-3.1-8B multi-model test ✓ covered\n- exp-008: RE N=16 confirmation ✓ covered\n- exp-009: Capability stratification by F1 buckets ✓ covered\n- exp-010: Selection curves for N=1,2,4,8,16 ✓ covered\n- exp-011: OOD robustness (WNUT model on unseen domain) ✓ covered\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Are the metrics (SJ, FK) well-defined? Is Spearman ρ the right evaluation measure? Are there methodological gaps?\n\n2. **Experimental Design**: Is the experimental matrix comprehensive enough? Are the baselines appropriate? Are there confounds not controlled for?\n\n3. **Statistical Rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is the reporting of significance adequate? Any concerns about multiple comparisons, sample sizes, or effect sizes?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Missing hyperparameters, training details?\n\n5. **Specific Concerns**: \n   - Table 1 uses T=1.0 for SciERC but T=0.7 for CoNLL/WNUT—is this a fair comparison?\n   - Only 3 seeds for N=16 stability—is this sufficient?\n   - The paper uses only 8B models—how much does this limit generalizability?\n   - Is the inverted-U claim supported given non-significant trend tests?\n\n6. **Strengths and Weaknesses**: List top 3 strengths and top 3 weaknesses.\n\n7. **Score**: Provide your preliminary score (1-6) with justification.\n\n8. **Questions for Authors**: List 3-5 questions you would ask.\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers. Be rigorous but fair—this is a negative-result paper which inherently faces higher scrutiny on thoroughness.","agentId":"a2271c80e6d8c2759","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe review covers the \"worst\" tier paper, \"Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal for Structured Prediction,\" which presents a predominantly negative result on using SJ and FK as confidence estimators for IE across 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, and 2 model families.\n\n**Top strengths identified:**\n1. Comprehensive experimental matrix with honest negative-result framing (11 experiments covering datasets, models, temperatures, N-scaling, stratification, OOD, selection curves)\n2. Three failure modes framework (ceiling, floor, model-specific confounds) provides genuinely useful diagnostic categories\n3. Best-of-N selection experiment (SJ vs. random p=0.47) delivers the definitive practical test\n\n**Top weaknesses identified:**\n1. **Missing critical baselines** — no comparison to log-probability, exact-match voting, or Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024). Without these, the paper cannot distinguish \"consistency fails for structured prediction\" from \"SJ and FK are poor operationalizations\"\n2. **Temperature confound in the cross-dataset table** — T=1.0 for SciERC but T=0.7 for CoNLL/WNUT undermines the non-transferability argument\n3. **Formula-implementation discrepancy** — paper describes symmetric max-match; code uses type-grouped Hungarian matching with different denominator\n4. **Inverted-U pattern lacks statistical support** — Jonckheere-Terpstra tests non-significant (p=0.12, p=0.38) yet the pattern drives the discussion and practitioner guidelines\n5. **Only 3 seeds** and **Spearman rho as sole evaluation metric** limit the statistical conclusions\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":254125,"totalTokens":102761,"totalToolUseCount":26,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5942,"cache_read_input_tokens":96330,"output_tokens":488,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5942},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":488,"cache_read_input_tokens":96330,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5942,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5942,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
